/*
 * Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology (1BI0) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real Pearson Edexcel GCSE Biology (1BI0) questions are
 * context-led: a student's investigation, a real-world application, a table of
 * results or a described graph opens each question, practicals are examined in
 * context, and maths skills run throughout. Every item here follows that
 * pattern, written at both Foundation-overlap and Higher demand.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: one marking point per line with (1), allow / ignore
 * / do not accept notes, max n; 6-mark extended responses use the board's three-
 * level ladder with indicative content.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const BIOLOGY_EDEXCEL_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  '1': {
    name: 'Key Concepts in Biology',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about bacteria.\n\nSome bacteria can move through liquids towards a source of food. These bacteria have a long, whip-like structure attached to the outside of the cell.\n\nName this structure.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `flagellum / flagella (1)\nAccept tail\nIgnore cilia` },
      { q: `Ribosomes are very small sub-cellular structures. The diameter of a ribosome is about 25 nm.\n\nConvert 25 nm into micrometres (µm).`, marks: 1, command: 'Convert', markScheme: `0.025 (µm) (1)\nAccept 2.5 × 10⁻² (µm)` },
      { q: `A student wanted to find out if a sample of cooked rice contained starch.\n\nThe student crushed the rice with a little water in a pestle and mortar.\n\nName the reagent the student should add to the rice to test for starch.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `iodine (solution) (1)\nAccept potassium iodide / iodine in potassium iodide\nDo not accept Benedict's / Biuret` },
      { q: `A student used a light microscope to look at a prepared slide of onion epidermis cells.\n\nThe teacher told the student to focus on the cells using the lowest power objective lens first.\n\nGive one reason why the student should start with the lowest power objective lens.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• larger field of view / easier to find the cells (1)\n• easier to focus / less chance of the lens hitting (and breaking) the slide (1)\nIgnore to see the cells more clearly` },
      { q: `Root hair cells take in nitrate ions from the soil by active transport.\n\nState the process in the root hair cell that provides the energy for active transport.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(aerobic) respiration (1)\nAccept ATP from respiration\nDo not accept photosynthesis` },
      { q: `This question is about digestive enzymes.\n\nLipase is an enzyme made in the pancreas. Lipase breaks down lipids in the small intestine.\n\nName the products of the breakdown of lipids by lipase.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `fatty acids and glycerol (1)\nBoth needed for the mark\nDo not accept amino acids / sugars` },
      { q: `Muscle cells in the leg contract to move the body. Muscle cells contain many mitochondria.\n\nExplain why muscle cells contain many mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• mitochondria are the site of (aerobic) respiration (1)\n• (respiration) releases (a lot of) energy (needed) for (muscle) contraction (1)\nDo not accept 'produces / makes energy' for the second mark\nIgnore 'powerhouse of the cell' unqualified` },
      { q: `Figure 1 shows two cells from the same plant (described for this question): a palisade cell from a leaf, containing many chloroplasts, and a root hair cell, which contains no chloroplasts.\n\nExplain why root hair cells do not contain chloroplasts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• roots are underground / root hair cells do not receive light (1)\n• so (root hair cells) cannot carry out photosynthesis / chloroplasts would have no function (1)\nAccept chloroplasts absorb light (energy) for photosynthesis for 1 mark if neither point given` },
      { q: `A sperm cell is a specialised cell. At the tip of the head of a sperm cell is a structure called the acrosome.\n\nExplain how the acrosome helps the sperm cell to carry out its function.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the acrosome contains (digestive) enzymes (1)\n• (enzymes) break down / digest the outer layer / jelly coat / membrane of the egg cell, so the sperm can enter / penetrate the egg (1)\nIgnore 'to fertilise the egg' unqualified` },
      { q: `An egg cell is a specialised cell.\n\nImmediately after one sperm cell fuses with an egg cell, the cell membrane of the egg cell changes.\n\nDescribe how the egg cell membrane changes and why this is important.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the membrane hardens / becomes impermeable (to sperm) (1)\n• so that no other sperm can enter / only one sperm fertilises the egg (1)\nAccept so that the zygote / fertilised egg has the correct number of chromosomes / is diploid` },
      { q: `The trachea is lined with ciliated epithelial cells. Other cells in the lining of the trachea make sticky mucus.\n\nExplain how ciliated epithelial cells help to protect the lungs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cilia beat / move / wave (1)\n• moving mucus (containing trapped dust / pathogens / bacteria) up and away from the lungs / to the throat (1)\nIgnore cilia 'trap' dust or pathogens\nDo not accept cilia kill pathogens` },
      { q: `Cells use enzymes to build large molecules. For example, protein molecules are made by joining amino acids together.\n\nExplain why enzymes are needed for the synthesis of large molecules such as proteins in cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• enzymes are (biological) catalysts / speed up reactions (1)\n• without enzymes these reactions would be too slow (at body temperature) to sustain life / to make enough protein (for growth / repair) (1)\nAccept so reactions can happen at body temperature for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student prepared a slide of cheek cells and looked at them using a light microscope.\n\nThe student drew one of the cells. The width of the cell in the drawing was 36 mm. The actual width of the cell was 60 µm.\n\nCalculate the magnification of the drawing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• conversion to the same units: 36 mm = 36 000 µm or 60 µm = 0.06 mm (1)\n• (36 000 ÷ 60 =) (×) 600 (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nDo not accept 0.6 or 600 000 for the second mark` },
      { q: `A scientist took an electron micrograph of a chloroplast at a magnification of ×8000.\n\nThe length of the chloroplast in the micrograph was 40 mm.\n\nCalculate the actual length of the chloroplast in micrometres (µm).\n\nUse the equation:\nactual size = image size ÷ magnification`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 40 ÷ 8000 = 0.005 (mm) (1)\n• (0.005 mm × 1000 =) 5 (µm) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 5 × 10⁻⁶ m` },
      { q: `This question is about the sizes of microorganisms (data written for this practice question).\n\nA bacterial cell is 3 µm long. A virus particle is 120 nm long.\n\nCalculate how many times longer the bacterial cell is than the virus particle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• conversion: 3 µm = 3000 nm or 120 nm = 0.12 µm (1)\n• (3000 ÷ 120 =) 25 (times) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student looked at plant cells using a light microscope with a total magnification of ×400.\n\nThe diameter of the field of view was 0.4 mm. The student counted 8 cells lying end to end across the diameter of the field of view.\n\nCalculate the mean width of one cell in micrometres (µm).`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.4 ÷ 8 = 0.05 (mm) (1)\n• (0.05 × 1000 =) 50 (µm) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `The resolution of a light microscope is about 200 nm. Ribosomes are about 25 nm in diameter.\n\nExplain why ribosomes can be seen using an electron microscope but not using a light microscope.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ribosomes are smaller than the resolution of a light microscope / smaller than 200 nm (1)\n• an electron microscope has a higher resolution / can distinguish between two points that are closer together (1)\nIgnore electron microscope has a higher magnification unqualified` },
      { q: `A student looked at cells from a moss leaf using a light microscope and made a drawing of one cell.\n\nThe student's drawing was very small in one corner of the page, used shading to show the chloroplasts and had no labels.\n\nGive two ways the student could improve this drawing so that it is a scientific drawing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• draw it larger / fill at least half the space (1)\n• no shading / use clear, single (unbroken) lines (1)\n• add labels (of structures) with ruled label lines (1)\n• add a title / state the magnification / add a scale (1)\nIgnore 'make it neater' unqualified` },
      { q: `Amylase is an enzyme that breaks down starch. A student added amylase to a solution of the protein albumin. After 30 minutes the albumin had not been broken down.\n\nExplain why amylase does not break down albumin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• enzymes are specific / the active site (of amylase) has a specific shape (1)\n• albumin / protein is not complementary to the active site / does not fit into the active site (1)\nDo not accept amylase is denatured\nDo not accept active site is the same shape as the substrate` },
      { q: `A student investigated the activity of the enzyme catalase. Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.\n\nThe student collected 36 cm³ of oxygen in 1.5 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production in cm³ per second.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1.5 minutes = 90 s (1)\n• (36 ÷ 90 =) 0.4 (cm³ per second) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 24 (cm³ per minute)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of pH on the activity of amylase. At pH 6, the time taken for all of the starch to be broken down was 150 s.\n\nThe student calculated the rate of reaction using the equation:\nrate = 1000 ÷ time\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at pH 6. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1000 ÷ 150 (= 6.666…) (1)\n• 6.7 (s⁻¹) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 6.67 / 6.666… not to 2 significant figures` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of pH on the activity of amylase. The student mixed starch solution, amylase solution and a pH buffer in test tubes. All the test tubes were placed in a water bath at 35 °C.\n\nExplain why the student placed all the test tubes in a water bath at 35 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• temperature affects (the rate of) enzyme activity (1)\n• (keeping temperature the same) so that only pH affects the rate / it is a fair test / temperature is a control variable (1)\nAccept 35 °C is close to the optimum temperature (for amylase) for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A food scientist wanted to find out if a sample of cheese contained fat.\n\nThe scientist crushed a small piece of the cheese.\n\nDescribe how the scientist could use the ethanol emulsion test to show that the cheese contained fat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• add ethanol (to the crushed cheese), shake, then add (the liquid to) water (1)\n• a cloudy (white) emulsion forms (if fat is present) (1)\nDo not accept 'turns white' with no reference to cloudiness / emulsion` },
      { q: `A student made a solution from a crushed grape.\n\nDescribe how the student should test this solution for a reducing sugar, and give the result if a reducing sugar is present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• add Benedict's (solution / reagent) and heat in a (hot) water bath (1)\n• colour changes from blue to green / yellow / orange / brick-red (1)\nAccept heat to 80 °C or above\nDo not accept 'boil' using a Bunsen burner directly\nAccept red for brick-red` },
      { q: `A student used calorimetry to find the energy in a piece of bread. The student burned the bread under a boiling tube containing 25 g of water.\n\nThe temperature of the water increased from 21 °C to 47 °C.\n\nCalculate the energy transferred to the water.\n\nUse the equation:\nenergy (J) = mass of water (g) × temperature change (°C) × 4.2`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• temperature change = 47 − 21 = 26 (°C) (1)\n• (25 × 26 × 4.2 =) 2730 (J) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAccept 2.73 kJ` },
      { q: `A student used calorimetry to measure the energy in a peanut. The student burned the peanut under a boiling tube of water and measured the temperature rise of the water.\n\nThe student's value for the energy in the peanut was much lower than the value on the food label.\n\nGive two reasons why the student's value was lower.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• energy / heat lost to the surroundings / air (1)\n• energy used to heat the boiling tube / thermometer (1)\n• peanut not completely burned / some peanut left unburned (1)\n• peanut cooled while being moved from the flame to under the tube (1)\nIgnore human error unqualified` },
      { q: `Oxygen enters cells in the body by diffusion.\n\nExplain why there is a net movement of oxygen into a respiring body cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• oxygen is used up in (aerobic) respiration (in the cell) (1)\n• so there is a lower concentration of oxygen inside the cell than outside / oxygen moves down its concentration gradient (1)\nDo not accept oxygen moves against a concentration gradient` },
      { q: `A student investigated osmosis in potato tissue. The student put a potato cylinder into a concentrated sucrose solution for 30 minutes.\n\nThe mass of the potato cylinder at the start was 2.50 g. The mass at the end was 2.15 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in mass of the potato cylinder.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• change in mass = 2.15 − 2.50 = −0.35 (g) (1)\n• (−0.35 ÷ 2.50 × 100 =) −14 (%) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAccept 14% decrease / loss\nAllow 1 mark for 14 with no sign or indication of loss` },
      { q: `A scientist examined a cell taken from a pond (written for this practice question). The cell had:\n• a cell wall\n• a cell membrane\n• ribosomes\n• chromosomal DNA in the cytoplasm, not in a nucleus\n• small rings of plasmid DNA.\n\nExplain whether this cell is a plant cell or a bacterial cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• bacterial cell (1)\n• (because) the DNA is not in a nucleus / there is no nucleus / it is prokaryotic (1)\n• (because) it has plasmids / plasmid DNA (1)\nAccept there are no chloroplasts / no (permanent) vacuole (listed)\nThe first mark can only be awarded with at least one correct reason\nIgnore it has a cell wall (both types have one)` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a bacterium was taken at a magnification of 2.5 × 10⁴.\n\nThe length of the bacterium in the micrograph was 50 mm.\n\nCalculate the actual length of the bacterium in metres. Give your answer in standard form.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 50 ÷ 25 000 (1)\n• = 0.002 (mm) / 2 (µm) (1)\n• 2 × 10⁻⁶ (m) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow 2 marks for 0.000 002 m (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `A student measured the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe rate was 4.2 arbitrary units at 40 °C and 0.0 arbitrary units at 60 °C.\n\nExplain why the rate of reaction at 60 °C was 0.0 arbitrary units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (at 60 °C) the enzyme is denatured (1)\n• the shape of the active site has changed (1)\n• so the substrate no longer fits / is no longer complementary to the active site / no enzyme-substrate complexes form (1)\nDo not accept the enzyme is killed / dies\nIgnore bonds break unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of hydrogen peroxide concentration on the activity of catalase. The volume of catalase solution was kept the same.\n\nThe results (data written for this practice question) were:\nconcentration of hydrogen peroxide (%) — rate of oxygen production (cm³ per minute)\n0.5 — 4\n1.0 — 8\n2.0 — 15\n3.0 — 18\n4.0 — 18\n\nExplain the effect of increasing the concentration of hydrogen peroxide on the rate of reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (from 0.5% to 3.0%) rate increases as concentration increases (1)\n• because there are more (successful) collisions between substrate and active sites / more enzyme-substrate complexes form (1)\n• (above 3.0%) rate stays constant because all the active sites are occupied / enzyme is saturated / enzyme concentration is the limiting factor (1)\nAccept use of data in place of description of trend` },
      { q: `A student put one potato cylinder into distilled water and another potato cylinder into 1.0 mol dm⁻³ sucrose solution. Both were left for 24 hours.\n\nThe cylinder in distilled water increased in mass. The cylinder in 1.0 mol dm⁻³ sucrose solution decreased in mass.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• water moves by osmosis across a partially permeable membrane (1)\n• in distilled water, water moves into the potato cells because the solution outside is more dilute than the cell contents / water moves from high to low water concentration (1)\n• in 1.0 mol dm⁻³ sucrose, water moves out of the potato cells because the solution is more concentrated than the cell contents (1)\nDo not accept sucrose moves into / out of the cells\nIgnore references to diffusion of water if osmosis is not named, max 2` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of potassium ions in the soil and in the root hair cells of a plant (data written for this practice question).\n\nconcentration of potassium ions in soil water: 0.2 mmol dm⁻³\nconcentration of potassium ions in root hair cells: 15.0 mmol dm⁻³\n\nWhen the soil was flooded and contained little oxygen, the uptake of potassium ions by the root hair cells decreased.\n\nExplain why the uptake of potassium ions decreased when the soil contained little oxygen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• potassium ions are absorbed by active transport / against the concentration gradient (from 0.2 to 15.0 mmol dm⁻³) (1)\n• active transport requires energy / ATP from respiration (1)\n• less oxygen means less aerobic respiration, so less energy is released (for active transport) (1)\nDo not accept energy is 'made' / 'produced'` },
      { q: `The first light microscopes were developed in the 1600s. Electron microscopes were developed in the 1930s.\n\nExplain how the development of the electron microscope has increased scientists' understanding of cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• electron microscopes have a higher resolution (than light microscopes) (1)\n• (and) a higher magnification (1)\n• so smaller (sub-cellular) structures / more detail can be seen, e.g. ribosomes / the internal structure of mitochondria or chloroplasts / cell membranes (1)\nAccept allowing scientists to work out the function of sub-cellular structures for the third mark` },
      { q: `A student used calorimetry to find the energy content of a crisp.\n\nThe student burned 0.80 g of crisp under a boiling tube containing 20 g of water. The temperature of the water increased from 19.0 °C to 61.0 °C.\n\nCalculate the energy released per gram of crisp.\n\nUse the equation:\nenergy (J) = mass of water (g) × temperature change (°C) × 4.2`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• temperature change = 61.0 − 19.0 = 42.0 (°C) (1)\n• energy = 20 × 42.0 × 4.2 = 3528 (J) (1)\n• (3528 ÷ 0.80 =) 4410 (J per g) (1)\nAward full marks for correct answer with no working\nAccept 4.41 kJ per g\nAllow ECF from an incorrect energy value` },
      { q: `A student investigated osmosis in potato. The student put potato cylinders into sucrose solutions of different concentrations for 1 hour and calculated the percentage change in mass of each cylinder.\n\nThe results (data written for this practice question) were:\nconcentration of sucrose solution (mol dm⁻³) — percentage change in mass (%)\n0.0 — +12.0\n0.2 — +5.6\n0.4 — −1.2\n0.6 — −7.8\n0.8 — −13.5\n1.0 — −16.0\n\nPlot a graph of these results, draw a line of best fit, and use your graph to estimate the concentration of sucrose solution that would cause no change in mass.`, marks: 4, command: 'Plot', diagram: true, markScheme: `• axes labelled with units: concentration of sucrose solution (mol dm⁻³) on x-axis, percentage change in mass (%) on y-axis, with a scale including negative values (1)\n• all points plotted correctly to within half a small square (1)\n• suitable (curved or straight) line of best fit (1)\n• estimate read from where the line crosses 0%: 0.36 (mol dm⁻³) (1)\nAccept any value in the range 0.33–0.40 mol dm⁻³ consistent with the candidate's line\nAllow 1 mark for plotting if 5 points correct` },
      { q: `Sperm cells are specialised to fertilise an egg cell. A sperm cell has a head containing a nucleus and an acrosome, a middle section and a long tail.\n\nExplain how the structure of a sperm cell is adapted to its function.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two pairs of linked points:\n• (long) tail / flagellum (1) to swim / move to the egg (1)\n• many mitochondria (in the middle section) (1) to release energy (from respiration) for the tail to move / for swimming (1)\n• acrosome (1) containing enzymes to digest the outer layer / membrane of the egg (1)\n• haploid nucleus / contains half the number of chromosomes (1) so the fertilised egg / zygote is diploid / has the full set of chromosomes (1)\nMax 4\nThe explanation mark must be linked to the correct feature` },
      { q: `*A student wanted to find the concentration of sucrose solution that is equal to the concentration of the cell contents of a potato.\n\nThe student was given a potato, a cork borer, a balance, paper towels, boiling tubes and sucrose solutions of concentrations 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and 1.0 mol dm⁻³.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to find the concentration of sucrose solution that is equal to the concentration of the cell contents of the potato.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nMethod\n• use a cork borer to cut potato cylinders of the same diameter and cut them to the same length\n• blot dry and measure the starting mass of each cylinder using a balance\n• put one cylinder into each sucrose concentration, using the same volume of solution in each boiling tube\n• leave for the same time, e.g. 30 minutes / 1 hour, at the same temperature\n• remove, blot dry with a paper towel (to remove surface solution) and reweigh\n• repeat at each concentration and calculate a mean\nProcessing\n• calculate the percentage change in mass = (final mass − starting mass) ÷ starting mass × 100\n• plot a graph of percentage change in mass against concentration of sucrose solution\n• the concentration where the line crosses 0% change (no net movement of water by osmosis) is equal to the concentration of the cell contents\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate; a basic method, e.g. put potato in different solutions and weigh it; lacks detail and structure.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant; a method that could be followed, with measurement of mass before and after and some control variables, but processing of results is limited.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout; a logical, detailed method with control variables and repeats, and a clear explanation of how percentage change and a graph are used to find the concentration at no change in mass.` },
      { q: `*A student investigated the effect of pH on the activity of amylase.\n\nThe student mixed amylase solution with starch solution and a buffer solution at 35 °C. Every 10 seconds the student put one drop of the mixture onto a spotting tile containing iodine solution. The student recorded the time taken until the iodine no longer turned blue-black.\n\nThe results (data written for this practice question) were:\npH — time taken for starch to be broken down (s)\n4 — no end point after 300 s\n5 — 120\n6 — 50\n7 — 70\n8 — 180\n\nExplain these results.\n\nInclude in your answer:\n• a description of the pattern in the results\n• how pH affects the enzyme\n• how the method could be improved to find the optimum pH more precisely.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nPattern\n• the time taken decreases from pH 5 to pH 6 and increases from pH 6 to pH 8\n• so the rate is highest / time is shortest at pH 6; the optimum pH is (about) pH 6\n• at pH 4 the starch was not broken down within 300 s\nExplanation\n• at the optimum pH the active site has the correct shape / is complementary to starch, so most enzyme-substrate complexes form\n• at pH values further from the optimum the shape of the active site changes\n• so starch fits less well / fewer enzyme-substrate complexes form / the rate is slower\n• at pH 4 the enzyme is denatured / the active site has changed shape so starch no longer fits\n• the blue-black colour shows starch is still present; the end point shows all the starch has been broken down (to maltose / sugar)\nImprovements\n• test more pH values between pH 5 and pH 7, e.g. pH 5.5, 6.0, 6.5\n• sample more often, e.g. every 5 seconds\n• repeat each pH and calculate a mean\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate; e.g. identifies pH 6 as fastest or states the enzyme is denatured, with little explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant; describes the pattern and links pH to a change in the active site, or gives a suitable improvement, with some structure.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout; a detailed, logically structured explanation that uses the data, links changes in pH to the shape of the active site and enzyme-substrate complexes, and gives a valid improvement for precision.` }
    ]
  },
  '2': {
    name: 'Cells and Control',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student looked at cells from an onion root tip using a light microscope.\nIn one cell, the chromosomes were lined up across the middle (equator) of the cell.\n\nName the stage of mitosis shown by this cell.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• metaphase (1)\nDo not accept: interphase / anaphase` },
      { q: `This question is about the brain.\nThe brain controls many processes in the body that happen without conscious thought.\n\nName the part of the brain that controls heart rate and breathing rate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• medulla oblongata (1)\nAccept: medulla\nDo not accept: cerebellum / brain stem alone` },
      { q: `When a person walks from a dark room into bright sunlight, their pupils become smaller.\n\nName the part of the eye that changes the size of the pupil.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• iris (1)\nDo not accept: cornea / lens` },
      { q: `A gardener cuts the tip off a shoot of a geranium plant. New shoots grow from the plant just below the cut.\nThese new shoots grow from cells that are undifferentiated.\n\nName the plant tissue that contains these undifferentiated cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• meristem / meristematic tissue (1)\nAccept: stem cells in the meristem\nDo not accept: xylem / phloem` },
      { q: `A person stands on a drawing pin. Receptors in the skin of the foot detect the pin.\n\nState the type of neurone that carries the electrical impulse from the receptor to the spinal cord.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• sensory neurone (1)\nDo not accept: relay neurone / motor neurone` },
      { q: `Neurones meet at junctions called synapses.\nThe gap at one synapse is 20 nm wide.\n1 nm = 1 × 10⁻⁹ m\n\nGive the width of this gap in metres, in standard form.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• 2 × 10⁻⁸ (m) (1)\nAccept: 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ m\nDo not accept: 20 × 10⁻⁹ m (not standard form)` },
      { q: `This question is about the cell cycle.\nBefore a cell divides by mitosis, it goes through a stage called interphase.\n\nDescribe two things that happen in a cell during interphase.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• DNA is replicated / copied (1)\n• the cell grows / increases in size (1)\n• (number of) organelles increase / mitochondria or ribosomes are made (1)\n• proteins are synthesised (1)\nAccept: chromosomes are copied to form two chromatids\nDo not accept: chromosomes line up / separate (these are stages of mitosis)` },
      { q: `A student counted the cells in each stage of the cell cycle in a stained onion root tip (data written for this practice question).\n\nStage — Number of cells\ninterphase — 172\nprophase — 14\nmetaphase — 6\nanaphase — 4\ntelophase — 4\n\nCalculate the percentage of the cells that were in mitosis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• cells in mitosis = 14 + 6 + 4 + 4 = 28 and total = 200 (1)\n• (28 ÷ 200) × 100 = 14 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAccept: 14.0%` },
      { q: `A skin cell divides by mitosis to replace a damaged skin cell.\n\nExplain why the two cells produced by mitosis are genetically identical.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• DNA / each chromosome is copied (exactly) before division / during interphase (1)\n• (one copy of) each chromosome / chromatid moves to each new cell / each cell receives the same set of chromosomes (1)\nAccept: diploid cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell for MP2\nDo not accept: references to meiosis or gametes` },
      { q: `Strawberry plants can reproduce asexually. They grow long stems called runners. New plants grow from the runners. The new plants are produced by mitosis.\nA farmer has a strawberry plant that produces large, sweet fruit.\n\nGive two advantages to the farmer of producing new strawberry plants from runners.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• new plants are genetically identical / clones of the parent (1)\n• so they will also produce large, sweet fruit / keep the desired characteristic (1)\n• only one parent plant is needed / no pollination needed (1)\n• it is quick / many new plants produced quickly (1)\n• cheap (1)` },
      { q: `Skin cancer can be caused by exposure to ultraviolet light from the Sun.\nUltraviolet light can cause changes (mutations) in the DNA of skin cells.\n\nExplain how a change in the DNA of a skin cell can lead to a tumour.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (mutation causes) changes in the cell cycle / the genes that control cell division (1)\n• (so) the cell divides (by mitosis) uncontrollably / too many times, forming a mass of cells (1)\nAccept: cell divides without stopping\nDo not accept: cells grow uncontrollably (without reference to division)` },
      { q: `A scientist grew some cancer cells in a laboratory. Each cell divided once every 20 hours (data written for this practice question).\nThe scientist started with one cancer cell.\n\nCalculate the number of cancer cells after 5 days.\nAssume no cells died.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = (5 × 24) ÷ 20 = 6 (1)\n• 2⁶ = 64 (cells) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAllow 1 mark for 2⁶ shown with an arithmetic error.` },
      { q: `Plants and animals both grow by cell division and cell differentiation.\nA bean seedling also grows longer because some of its cells become longer.\n\nDescribe two ways that growth in plants is different from growth in animals.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• plants grow by cell elongation / animals do not (1)\n• plant cells can differentiate throughout life / most animal cells differentiate only at an early stage (1)\n• plants keep growing throughout their life / animals stop growing when they reach adult size (1)\n• growth in plants occurs mainly at meristems / tips of shoots and roots, but in animals growth happens all over the body (1)\nAccept: plants have meristems, animals do not` },
      { q: `A human embryo starts as a single cell, the zygote. By birth, the baby contains many different types of cell, such as nerve cells, red blood cells and muscle cells.\n\nExplain why cell differentiation is important in the development of the baby.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cells become specialised / develop different structures (1)\n• so that they can carry out a particular function (efficiently) (1)\nAccept: cells form tissues / organs with different functions for MP2\nDo not accept: cells divide (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Doctors use percentile charts to monitor the growth of babies.\nThe table shows the mass at five percentiles for girls aged 6 months and 12 months (data written for this practice question).\n\nAge — 2nd — 25th — 50th — 75th — 98th\n6 months — 5.8 kg — 6.8 kg — 7.4 kg — 8.0 kg — 9.2 kg\n12 months — 7.0 kg — 8.2 kg — 8.9 kg — 9.7 kg — 11.0 kg\n\nA girl had a mass of 7.4 kg at 6 months and 7.6 kg at 12 months.\n\nUsing the table, explain the conclusion that a doctor could make about the growth of this girl.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• she has dropped from the 50th percentile to between the 2nd and 25th percentile (1)\n• so she is not gaining mass as expected / doctor may be concerned and investigate (her diet or health) (1)\nAccept: she is now lighter than (more than) 75% of girls her age\nDo not accept: she is underweight (without reference to percentiles)` },
      { q: `A baby boy had a mass of 3.4 kg at birth.\nAt 6 months, his mass was 7.6 kg.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the mass of this baby.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (7.6 − 3.4) = 4.2 and 4.2 ÷ 3.4 (× 100) (1)\n• 124 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAllow 1 mark for 123.5 (not rounded) or 224 (%)` },
      { q: `Type 1 diabetes is caused when the cells in the pancreas that make insulin are destroyed.\nScientists are investigating the use of stem cells to replace these cells.\n\nExplain why embryonic stem cells may be more useful than adult stem cells for this treatment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• embryonic stem cells can differentiate into any type of cell / are pluripotent (1)\n• adult stem cells can only differentiate into a limited number of cell types / cannot form pancreatic cells (1)\nAccept: embryonic stem cells are easier to grow / divide more for MP2` },
      { q: `In a clinical trial, stem cells were injected into the brains of patients with Parkinson's disease. The stem cells were grown in a laboratory before being injected.\n\nGive two risks of using stem cells to treat this disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• stem cells may be rejected (by the immune system) (1)\n• stem cells may divide uncontrollably / form tumours / cause cancer (1)\n• stem cells may be infected with a virus / pathogen and pass it on (1)\n• stem cells may differentiate into the wrong type of cell (1)` },
      { q: `A gardener takes a cutting from a rose plant. The cut end of the stem is placed in moist compost. After a few weeks, roots grow from the stem.\n\nExplain how stem cells in the cutting allow new roots to grow.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• meristem cells / stem cells in plants can divide (by mitosis) (1)\n• and differentiate into any type of plant cell / into root cells (1)\nAccept: plant stem cells can differentiate throughout the life of the plant` },
      { q: `Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a disease in which the myelin sheath around the axons of neurones is damaged.\nPeople with MS often have slow reactions.\n\nExplain why damage to the myelin sheath causes slow reactions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the myelin sheath insulates the axon / makes impulses jump between gaps in the sheath (1)\n• (so without it) impulses travel more slowly (along the neurone) (1)\nAccept: impulses may not be transmitted / may leak\nDo not accept: messages travel slowly (must refer to impulses for MP2)` },
      { q: `An electrical impulse travels along a motor neurone from the spinal cord to a muscle in the foot.\nThe neurone is 0.9 m long. The impulse takes 0.015 s to travel along the neurone.\n\nCalculate the speed of the impulse.\nUse the equation:\nspeed = distance ÷ time`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.9 ÷ 0.015 (1)\n• 60 (m/s) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `Some snake venoms contain a chemical that attaches to the receptors on the membrane of a muscle cell at the synapse with a motor neurone. The neurotransmitter can no longer attach to these receptors.\n\nExplain why a person bitten by one of these snakes may be unable to move.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• neurotransmitter cannot bind to receptors / cannot diffuse to receptors and attach (1)\n• (so) no impulse is passed on / muscle does not receive an impulse so does not contract (1)\nAccept: no impulse in the next cell for MP2` },
      { q: `A student investigated reaction time using the ruler drop test. Another person dropped a ruler and the student caught it. The distance the ruler fell before it was caught was recorded (data written for this practice question).\n\nAttempt — 1 — 2 — 3 — 4 — 5\nDistance in cm — 12 — 13 — 27 — 14 — 11\n\nCalculate the mean distance the ruler fell.\nDo not include the anomalous result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (12 + 13 + 14 + 11) ÷ 4 / identifies 27 as anomalous (1)\n• 12.5 (cm) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAllow 1 mark for 15.4 (cm) (anomaly included)` },
      { q: `A man had a stroke. Part of his cerebellum was damaged.\n\nDescribe two effects this damage could have on the man.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• loss of balance (1)\n• poor coordination of movement / clumsy movements (1)\n• difficulty with muscle control / fine movements such as writing (1)\n• difficulty walking / standing (1)\nDo not accept: loss of memory / speech (cerebral hemispheres)` },
      { q: `Doctors want to find out which parts of a patient's brain are active when the patient is speaking.\nThey can use either a CT scan or a PET scan.\n\nExplain why a PET scan would be more useful than a CT scan for this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a PET scan shows (brain) activity / which areas are using more glucose / are more active (1)\n• a CT scan only shows structure / tissues / cannot show activity (1)\nAccept: PET uses a radioactive tracer that builds up in active areas` },
      { q: `A student walks outside at night. The student finds it difficult to tell the colour of the flowers in a garden, but can still see their shapes.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to see colour in dim light.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cones (detect colour and) need bright light / do not work in dim light (1)\n• rods work in dim light but do not detect colour / only give black and white vision (1)\nDo not accept: rods and cones in the lens / cornea` },
      { q: `A girl cannot see the board clearly from the back of her classroom. She can read her book clearly. An optician tells her that she is short-sighted.\n\nExplain how wearing glasses with concave lenses corrects short-sightedness.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• concave lens makes light rays diverge / spread out (before they enter the eye) (1)\n• so light is focused on the retina (and not in front of it) (1)\nDo not accept: lens bends light more / converges light` },
      { q: `An elderly man has a cataract in one eye. Everything he sees with this eye looks cloudy.\n\nDescribe how a cataract can be treated.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• surgery to remove the (cloudy) lens (1)\n• replaced with an artificial / plastic lens (1)\nDo not accept: glasses / contact lenses / laser surgery on the cornea` },
      { q: `When a person touches a hot object, an electrical impulse travels along a sensory neurone to a synapse with a relay neurone in the spinal cord.\n\nDescribe how the impulse is passed from the sensory neurone to the relay neurone.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• neurotransmitter is released (from the end of the sensory neurone) (1)\n• neurotransmitter diffuses across the gap / synapse (1)\n• neurotransmitter binds to receptors on the relay neurone, causing a new impulse (1)\nAccept: chemical messenger for neurotransmitter\nDo not accept: impulse jumps across the gap` },
      { q: `A person picks up a very hot plate. They drop it before they feel any pain.\nThis is a reflex action.\n\nExplain why this reflex action is faster than a response that involves conscious thought.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• impulse goes (to the spinal cord and) does not go to the brain / does not involve the conscious part of the brain (1)\n• passes through a relay neurone (directly to a motor neurone) (1)\n• (so) fewer neurones / synapses involved / shorter pathway (1)\nAccept: response is automatic / no decision needs to be made` },
      { q: `A scientist examined 200 cells from a root tip. 28 of the cells were in mitosis (data written for this practice question).\nThe percentage of cells in a stage is proportional to the time spent in that stage.\nOne complete cell cycle takes 24 hours.\n\nCalculate the time these cells spend in mitosis.\nGive your answer in hours to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 28 ÷ 200 = 0.14 / 14% (1)\n• 0.14 × 24 = 3.36 (1)\n• 3.4 (hours) (1)\nAward 3 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAllow 2 marks for 3.36 not rounded.` },
      { q: `Parkinson's disease is a disorder of the brain. Researchers find it difficult to develop treatments for brain disorders.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to treat damage and disease in the brain.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• brain is protected by the skull / surgery is difficult (1)\n• brain tissue is delicate / easily damaged by surgery or treatment (1)\n• many drugs cannot get into the brain (from the blood) (1)\n• it is difficult to know which part of the brain is affected / how the brain works is not fully understood (1)\n• damaged neurones are not easily repaired / replaced (1)` },
      { q: `A woman finds that when she reads a book, the words look blurred. She can see distant objects clearly.\n\nExplain the cause of her problem and how it can be corrected.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• she is long-sighted (1)\n• light (from near objects) is focused behind the retina / eyeball too short / lens cannot bend light enough (1)\n• corrected by convex lens (in glasses or contact lenses) / laser surgery (1)\nAccept: converging lens for convex` },
      { q: `Some scientists want to use embryonic stem cells from spare embryos left over after IVF treatment to develop new medical treatments.\n\nExplain why some people object to the use of embryonic stem cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the embryo is destroyed (when stem cells are removed) (1)\n• some people believe an embryo is a (potential) human life / has a right to life (1)\n• religious objections / embryos cannot give consent (1)\nAccept: fear of misuse (e.g. cloning) for MP3` },
      { q: `A malignant tumour formed in the lungs of a patient. Some time later, the patient was found to have tumours in the liver and brain.\n\nExplain how the tumours formed in the liver and brain.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cells break off from the (malignant) tumour (1)\n• are carried in the blood / lymph to other parts of the body (1)\n• where they divide uncontrollably / by mitosis to form secondary tumours (1)\nDo not accept: tumour spreads (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether practice affects reaction time. The student caught a dropped ruler five times and recorded the distance it fell (data written for this practice question).\n\nTrial — 1 — 2 — 3 — 4 — 5\nDistance in cm — 18 — 15 — 13 — 12 — 12\n\nThe student concluded that practice decreases reaction time.\n\nEvaluate this conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• supports: distance (and so reaction time) decreases from 18 cm to 12 cm with practice (1)\n• but there is no further decrease after trial 4 / levels off at 12 cm (1)\n• only one student tested / each trial done only once / no repeats, so conclusion may not be valid for others (1)` },
      { q: `Some students think that listening to loud music increases reaction time.\nA ruler drop test can be used to measure reaction time.\n\nDevise a method to investigate the effect of listening to loud music on reaction time.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `• drop ruler (from same height / zero at the top of the person's thumb and finger) and catch, with and without loud music (1)\n• record the distance fallen / convert to reaction time using a table (1)\n• repeat (at least 3 times) and calculate a mean (1)\n• control variable, e.g. same person / same hand / same ruler / same volume of music / no warning of drop (1)\nAccept: use of an online reaction timer instead of a ruler` },
      { q: `Sensory neurones carry electrical impulses from receptors in the skin to the spinal cord.\nA sensory neurone has dendrites, a long dendron, a cell body, a short axon and a myelin sheath.\n\nExplain how the structure of a sensory neurone is related to its function.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• dendrites receive impulses from the receptor (1)\n• long dendron carries impulses (from the receptor) towards the cell body / over a long distance (1)\n• axon carries impulses away from the cell body (to the spinal cord / relay neurone) (1)\n• myelin sheath insulates the neurone (1)\n• so impulses travel faster (1)\n• nerve endings / axon terminals release neurotransmitter at the synapse (1)` },
      { q: `Stem cells could be used to treat many diseases. Stem cells can come from embryos, from adult tissue such as bone marrow, and from the meristems of plants.\n\n*Discuss the potential benefits and risks of using stem cells in medicine and in plant growing.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• embryonic stem cells can differentiate into any type of cell\n• could replace damaged cells, e.g. in type 1 diabetes, Parkinson's disease, spinal injury\n• adult stem cells from bone marrow are already used (e.g. to treat leukaemia)\n• adult stem cells can only form a limited range of cells\n• stem cells from the patient's own body are less likely to be rejected\n• risks: rejection by the immune system / need for immunosuppressant drugs\n• risks: stem cells may divide uncontrollably / form tumours / cancer\n• risks: may transfer viruses / infection\n• ethical issues: embryos are destroyed\n• meristem cells can produce clones of plants quickly and cheaply / of rare or useful plants\n• clones all have the same genes so all may be affected by the same disease\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): a balanced discussion of benefits and risks linked to scientific ideas; logical, well-linked.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): benefits and risks described with some explanation; partially linked.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): isolated facts about stem cells; little linkage.\n0 marks: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `The tip of a plant root grows downwards through the soil. Cells just behind the tip divide by mitosis. Further back, the cells get longer and then become specialised.\n\n*Explain how cells in the root tip divide and how this leads to growth of the root.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• cells at the tip are meristem / stem cells (undifferentiated)\n• interphase: DNA replicated, cell grows, organelles copied\n• prophase: chromosomes condense / become visible, nuclear membrane breaks down\n• metaphase: chromosomes line up at the equator / on spindle fibres\n• anaphase: chromatids pulled to opposite poles of the cell\n• telophase: new nuclear membranes form around each set of chromosomes\n• cytokinesis: cytoplasm and cell membrane divide, forming two genetically identical daughter cells\n• more cells increases the length of the root\n• cell elongation: cells behind the tip get longer, pushing the tip down\n• differentiation into specialised cells, e.g. root hair cells, xylem\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of the stages of mitosis linked to growth by elongation and differentiation; logical sequence.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some stages of the cell cycle described with some link to growth.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): isolated points about cell division or growth.\n0 marks: no rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '3': {
    name: 'Genetics',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about reproduction in plants.\n\nStrawberry plants grow long side stems called runners. New plants grow at the end of each runner. The new plants are genetically identical to the parent plant.\n\nState the type of reproduction shown by strawberry plants growing runners.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• asexual (reproduction) (1)\nIgnore vegetative / cloning\nDo not accept sexual` },
      { q: `Gametes are produced by meiosis.\n\nA human body cell contains 46 chromosomes.\n\nState the number of chromosomes in a human egg cell.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• 23 (1)\nAccept 23 single chromosomes / one from each pair\nDo not accept 23 pairs` },
      { q: `DNA is a polymer made of two strands. The strands are held together by complementary base pairs.\n\nName the base that pairs with cytosine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• guanine (1)\nAccept G\nDo not accept thymine / adenine` },
      { q: `This question is about protein synthesis.\n\nDuring transcription, the DNA of a gene is used as a template in the nucleus. The molecule that is made then moves out of the nucleus to a ribosome.\n\nName the molecule that is made during transcription.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• mRNA (1)\nAccept messenger RNA\nAccept RNA\nDo not accept tRNA / DNA` },
      { q: `A student extracted DNA from kiwi fruit. After filtering the mashed fruit, the student poured ice-cold ethanol slowly down the side of the test tube containing the filtrate.\n\nGive one reason why ethanol was added to the filtrate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• (DNA is insoluble in ethanol so) DNA precipitates / comes out of solution (1)\nAccept so that the DNA can be seen / forms white strands\nIgnore to separate the DNA unqualified` },
      { q: `Red-green colour blindness is a sex-linked disorder. It is caused by a recessive allele, Xᵇ, on the X chromosome. The dominant allele, Xᴮ, gives normal colour vision.\n\nDescribe the phenotype of a female with the genotype XᴮXᵇ.`, marks: 1, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• normal (colour) vision (1)\nAccept not colour blind\nIgnore carrier on its own (carrier is not a phenotype)\nDo not accept colour blind` },
      { q: `This question is about aphids.\n\nAphids are small insects that feed on plant sap. In summer, female aphids reproduce asexually and can produce many offspring very quickly. In autumn, aphids reproduce sexually.\n\nGive one advantage to aphids of asexual reproduction in summer and one advantage of sexual reproduction in autumn.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• asexual: only one parent needed / quicker / produces many offspring rapidly (to make use of the food available in summer) (1)\nAccept no need to find a mate / less energy used\n• sexual: produces variation in the offspring (so some may survive environmental change / winter conditions) (1)\nAccept offspring are genetically different` },
      { q: `This question is about cell division in the testes.\n\nCells in the testes divide by meiosis to produce sperm cells.\n\nDescribe two ways that the cells produced by meiosis are different from the cell that divided.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• they are haploid / have half the number of chromosomes (1)\nAccept have 23 chromosomes (in humans) / have one chromosome from each pair\n• they are genetically different (from the parent cell and from each other) (1)\nAccept they are not genetically identical\nIgnore they are smaller / there are four of them` },
      { q: `A student extracted DNA from strawberries.\n\nThe student mashed the strawberries in a beaker with a solution of washing-up liquid (detergent) and salt.\n\nExplain why detergent was added to the mashed strawberries.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• detergent breaks down (cell / nuclear) membranes (1)\nAccept breaks down lipids in membranes\n• so the DNA is released (from the nucleus / cell) (1)\nDo not accept breaks down cell walls` },
      { q: `A student extracted DNA from onion.\n\nThe mixture of chopped onion, detergent and salt solution was placed in a water bath at 60 °C for 15 minutes. The mixture was then cooled in an ice bath.\n\nExplain why the mixture was placed in a water bath at 60 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to denature enzymes (in the cells) (1)\n• that would break down the DNA (1)\nAccept to denature enzymes that would digest the DNA for 2 marks\nAccept speeds up the breakdown of membranes for 1 mark\nIgnore to kill the cells` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of each base in a sample of DNA from a cow (data written for this practice question).\n\nBase | Percentage (%)\nadenine (A) | 18\nthymine (T) | 18\ncytosine (C) | ?\nguanine (G) | ?\n\nCalculate the percentage of guanine in this DNA.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 100 − (18 + 18) = 64 (1)\n• 64 ÷ 2 = 32 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 64 seen\nIgnore units` },
      { q: `This question is about protein synthesis.\n\nTranslation takes place at a ribosome. Transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules are involved in translation.\n\nDescribe the role of tRNA in translation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• tRNA carries a specific amino acid (to the ribosome) (1)\n• the anticodon (on tRNA) binds to / is complementary to the codon on the mRNA (1)\nAccept three bases on tRNA pair with three bases on mRNA\nDo not accept tRNA makes the amino acid` },
      { q: `This question is about the genetic code.\n\nA section of mRNA that codes for a short protein is 213 bases long. Every three bases codes for one amino acid.\n\nCalculate the maximum number of amino acids that this section of mRNA could code for.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 213 ÷ 3 (1)\n• 71 (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `This question is about mutations.\n\nRNA polymerase binds to a region of non-coding DNA in front of a gene. A mutation occurs in this region of non-coding DNA.\n\nExplain how this mutation could reduce the amount of protein made from the gene.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• RNA polymerase is less able to bind / cannot bind (to the DNA) (1)\n• so less transcription / less mRNA is made (1)\nAccept so the gene is not transcribed / switched off\nIgnore the protein is a different shape` },
      { q: `Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inherited disorder. People with PKU have a mutation in the gene for an enzyme that breaks down an amino acid called phenylalanine. This enzyme does not work in people with PKU.\n\nExplain how a mutation in this gene could stop the enzyme from working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the (shape of the) active site changes (1)\nAccept the enzyme / protein has a different shape\n• so the substrate / phenylalanine no longer fits / binds (1)\nAccept no enzyme–substrate complexes can form\nDo not accept the enzyme is denatured / killed` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel carried out breeding experiments with pea plants and published his results in 1866. The importance of his work was not recognised until after his death.\n\nGive two reasons why the importance of Mendel's work was not recognised during his lifetime.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• chromosomes / genes / DNA had not been discovered (so there was no mechanism to explain his results) (1)\n• he was not well known / was a monk, not a recognised scientist (1)\n• he published in a little-known journal / few scientists read his work (1)\n• his ideas went against the accepted idea (of blending inheritance) (1)\n• other scientists did not understand / repeat the mathematics of his work (1)` },
      { q: `In pea plants, the allele for tall stems (T) is dominant to the allele for short stems (t).\n\nA pea plant has the genotype Tt.\n\nExplain the difference between the genotype and the phenotype of this pea plant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• genotype is the alleles (the plant has) / Tt (1)\n• phenotype is the characteristic shown / tall (1)\nThe answer must refer to this plant for 2 marks; general definitions only = 1 mark\nDo not accept genotype is the genes / phenotype is the appearance of genes` },
      { q: `In rabbits, the allele for brown fur (B) is dominant to the allele for white fur (b).\n\nA heterozygous brown rabbit was crossed with a white rabbit. Twelve offspring were produced.\n\nCalculate the number of offspring that would be expected to have white fur.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Bb × bb gives a probability of white of 0.5 / 50% / 1 in 2 (1)\nAccept Bb and bb in a ratio of 1:1\n• 0.5 × 12 = 6 (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele (f). The dominant allele is F.\n\nTwo parents are both heterozygous (Ff) for cystic fibrosis.\n\nCalculate the probability that their child will be a carrier of cystic fibrosis. Give your answer as a percentage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• offspring genotypes FF, Ff, Ff, ff / 2 out of 4 are Ff (1)\nAccept correct Punnett square\n• 50 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nDo not accept 75% (this includes FF)` },
      { q: `A couple are expecting their first baby.\n\nExplain why there is a 50% chance that the baby will be a boy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (all) eggs contain an X chromosome (1)\n• half of the sperm contain an X chromosome and half contain a Y chromosome (1)\nAccept male is XY and female is XX for 1 mark if neither marking point is given\nAccept a correct genetic diagram showing XX × XY giving XX, XX, XY, XY for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A family pedigree for an inherited disorder is described below.\n\nGeneration 1: Peter and Joan are both unaffected.\nGeneration 2: Their children are Sam, who is affected, and Ella, who is unaffected.\n\nExplain how this family pedigree shows that the disorder is caused by a recessive allele.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• neither parent / Peter nor Joan is affected but their child Sam is affected (1)\n• so both parents must be (heterozygous) carriers / each parent passed on a recessive allele (that was hidden by the dominant allele) (1)\nAccept if it were dominant, at least one parent would be affected` },
      { q: `The ABO blood group gene has three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and Iᴼ. The alleles Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant. The allele Iᴼ is recessive.\n\nA person has the genotype IᴬIᴮ.\n\nExplain what is meant by the term codominant, using the blood group of this person.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• both alleles are expressed in the phenotype / neither allele is dominant over the other (1)\n• so the person has blood group AB / has both A and B antigens (on their red blood cells) (1)\nDo not accept the alleles blend` },
      { q: `A woman has blood group A with the genotype IᴬIᴬ. Her partner has blood group B with the genotype IᴮIᴼ.\n\nGive the possible blood groups of their children.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• AB (1)\n• A (1)\nAward 1 mark only if any other blood group (B or O) is also given\nAccept correct genotypes IᴬIᴮ and IᴬIᴼ linked to the correct blood groups` },
      { q: `Red-green colour blindness is caused by a recessive allele on the X chromosome. In the UK about 8% of males are red-green colour blind, but only about 0.5% of females.\n\nExplain why red-green colour blindness is more common in males than in females.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• males have only one X chromosome / are XY (1)\n• so only one recessive allele is needed / there is no second (dominant) allele to mask it (1)\nAccept females need two recessive alleles / females can be carriers\nAccept the Y chromosome does not carry the gene` },
      { q: `Identical twins develop from the same fertilised egg. A pair of identical twins were separated at birth and raised in different families. At the age of 30, one twin had a mass of 64 kg and the other twin had a mass of 79 kg.\n\nExplain why the twins have different masses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• identical twins have the same DNA / genes / alleles (1)\n• so the difference is caused by the environment (1)\nAccept environmental factor named, e.g. diet / amount of exercise\nDo not accept genetic variation / mutation as the main cause` },
      { q: `This question is about mutations.\n\nMutations are changes in the sequence of bases in DNA. Mutations happen continuously, but most mutations have no effect on the phenotype of an organism.\n\nGive two reasons why most mutations have no effect on the phenotype.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• the mutation is in non-coding DNA (that does not affect a gene) (1)\n• the new triplet / codon still codes for the same amino acid (1)\n• the (shape / activity of the) protein is not changed / changed only slightly (1)\n• the mutated allele is recessive (so is masked by the dominant allele) (1)` },
      { q: `The Human Genome Project was completed in 2003. Scientists identified the sequence of bases in human DNA and the positions of human genes.\n\nGive two ways that the Human Genome Project can benefit medicine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• identifying genes / alleles linked to (inherited) disorders / diseases (1)\n• testing people for the risk of developing a disease (e.g. some cancers) (1)\nAccept earlier diagnosis\n• allowing personalised medicine / choosing the best drug or dose for a person (1)\n• developing new treatments / drugs / gene therapy (1)` },
      { q: `Almost all bananas sold in the UK are the Cavendish variety. Cavendish banana plants cannot produce seeds and are grown only from cuttings taken from other plants. A fungal disease called Panama disease is spreading through Cavendish banana plantations.\n\nExplain why Panama disease could destroy all Cavendish banana plants.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the plants are (genetically) identical / clones / there is no variation (1)\nAccept they are produced by asexual reproduction / mitosis\n• so if one plant is not resistant / is susceptible to the disease, none are (1)\nAccept no plants have resistance alleles` },
      { q: `In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick published a model of the structure of DNA. DNA is a polymer made from repeating units called nucleotides.\n\nDescribe the structure of a DNA molecule.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• (each nucleotide is made of) a sugar, a phosphate (group) and a base (1)\n• two strands (coiled to form) a double helix (1)\n• complementary base pairs / A pairs with T and C pairs with G (1)\n• (bases are) joined by (weak) hydrogen bonds (1)\n• the sugar–phosphate backbone is on the outside / alternating sugar and phosphate (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about protein synthesis.\n\nInsulin is a protein made in cells of the pancreas. The gene for insulin is found in the nucleus.\n\nDescribe how transcription of the insulin gene takes place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• RNA polymerase binds to (non-coding) DNA in front of the gene (1)\n• the two DNA strands separate / unzip (1)\nAccept hydrogen bonds break\n• (one strand is used as a template and) complementary (RNA) nucleotides / bases pair up (1)\n• mRNA is made / formed (1)\n• mRNA leaves the nucleus (through a nuclear pore) (1)\nDo not accept DNA leaves the nucleus` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel crossed pea plants that produced round seeds with plants that produced wrinkled seeds. All of the first-generation plants produced round seeds. He then crossed the first-generation plants with each other.\n\nIn the second generation, Mendel counted 5474 round seeds and 1850 wrinkled seeds.\n\nCalculate the ratio of round seeds to wrinkled seeds and explain why it is close to 3:1.\n\nGive your ratio in the form x:1, to two decimal places.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 5474 ÷ 1850 (1)\n• 2.96(:1) (1)\n• (explanation) the first-generation plants were heterozygous / Rr × Rr, so three genotypes in four have a dominant (round) allele (1)\nAccept ratio not exactly 3:1 because fertilisation is random / probabilities only predict results\nAward 2 marks for 2.96:1 with no working` },
      { q: `This question is about reproduction.\n\nIn sexual reproduction, two parents produce offspring that are genetically different from each other. In asexual reproduction, one parent produces offspring that are genetically identical.\n\nExplain why sexual reproduction produces genetic variation in the offspring.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• gametes are produced by meiosis (1)\n• (so) each gamete is genetically different / has a different combination of alleles (1)\n• fertilisation is random / any sperm can fuse with any egg (1)\n• (offspring receive) half of their alleles from each parent / a mixture of genes from two parents (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `Sickle cell disease is an inherited disorder. It is caused by a mutation in which one base in the gene for haemoglobin is changed.\n\nExplain how a change in one base in a gene can change the protein that is made.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the triplet / codon (of bases) is changed (1)\n• (so) a different amino acid is coded for / added (1)\n• (so) the protein folds differently / has a different shape (1)\nAccept the protein no longer works properly / has different activity for the third mark\nAccept the sequence of amino acids changes for the second mark` },
      { q: `The ABO blood group gene has three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and Iᴼ. The alleles Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant and the allele Iᴼ is recessive.\n\nA man with the genotype IᴬIᴼ and a woman with the genotype IᴮIᴼ have a child.\n\nUse a genetic diagram to calculate the probability that their child will have blood group O.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• gametes Iᴬ and Iᴼ from the man and Iᴮ and Iᴼ from the woman (1)\n• offspring genotypes IᴬIᴮ, IᴬIᴼ, IᴮIᴼ, IᴼIᴼ (1)\n• probability of blood group O = 0.25 / 25% / 1 in 4 / ¼ (1)\nAccept correct Punnett square for the first two marks\nDo not accept 1:4` },
      { q: `Haemophilia is a sex-linked disorder caused by a recessive allele, Xʰ, on the X chromosome. The dominant allele, Xᴴ, allows normal blood clotting.\n\nA woman who is a carrier of haemophilia (XᴴXʰ) and a man who does not have haemophilia (XᴴY) are planning to have a child.\n\nCalculate the probability that their child will be a boy with haemophilia.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• gametes Xᴴ and Xʰ from the mother and Xᴴ and Y from the father (1)\n• offspring genotypes XᴴXᴴ, XᴴXʰ, XᴴY, XʰY (1)\n• probability = 0.25 / 25% / 1 in 4 / ¼ (1)\nAccept correct Punnett square for the first two marks\nAllow 0.5 / 50% for 2 marks only if it is clearly the probability that a son has haemophilia` },
      { q: `A family pedigree for albinism is described below. Albinism is caused by a recessive allele (a). The dominant allele is A.\n\nGeneration 1: Raj and Priya do not have albinism.\nGeneration 2: Their first child, Arun, has albinism. Their second child, Maya, does not have albinism.\n\nRaj and Priya are expecting a third child.\n\nExplain the probability that their third child will have albinism.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Arun is aa so he received a recessive allele from each parent (1)\n• (so) Raj and Priya are both Aa / heterozygous / carriers (1)\n• (Aa × Aa) probability of aa = 0.25 / 25% / 1 in 4 (1)\nAccept the probability is the same for each child / previous children do not affect it` },
      { q: `In pea plants, the allele for yellow pods (A) is dominant to the allele for green pods (a).\n\nA heterozygous pea plant with yellow pods is crossed with a pea plant with green pods.\n\nDraw a Punnett square to show this cross and state the expected ratio of yellow-podded plants to green-podded plants in the offspring.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• parent genotypes Aa and aa (1)\n• gametes A and a from one parent and a and a from the other (1)\n• offspring genotypes Aa, Aa, aa, aa correctly shown in the Punnett square (1)\n• ratio yellow : green = 1:1 (1)\nAccept 2:2 / 50% yellow and 50% green\nAllow error carried forward for the ratio from an incorrect Punnett square` },
      { q: `This question is about protein synthesis.\n\nAmylase is an enzyme made in cells of the salivary glands. An mRNA molecule for amylase has moved from the nucleus to a ribosome in the cytoplasm.\n\nExplain how the sequence of bases in the mRNA determines the sequence of amino acids in amylase.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the ribosome reads the mRNA (in triplets / codons of three bases) (1)\n• each codon codes for a specific amino acid (1)\n• tRNA with a complementary anticodon binds to the codon (1)\n• tRNA brings / carries a specific amino acid (1)\n• amino acids are joined (in the order of the codons) to form a polypeptide / protein (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A class of students wanted to see DNA from fruit.\n\nThe teacher provided strawberries, washing-up liquid, salt, ice-cold ethanol, a water bath at 60 °C, an ice bath, filter funnels, filter paper and test tubes.\n\n*Describe how the students could extract DNA from the strawberries.\n\nInclude in your answer:\n• how to prepare the strawberries\n• how each of the substances is used\n• what the students would see.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• remove leaves / stalks and mash / crush the strawberries (with a pestle and mortar or in a plastic bag)\n• add a solution of washing-up liquid / detergent and salt\n• detergent breaks down (cell / nuclear) membranes to release DNA\n• salt causes the DNA to clump together\n• place in a water bath at 60 °C for about 15 minutes to denature enzymes that would break down DNA\n• then cool in an ice bath (to slow the breakdown of DNA)\n• filter the mixture and collect the filtrate in a test tube\n• pour ice-cold ethanol slowly down the side of the tube to form a layer on top of the filtrate\n• DNA is insoluble in (cold) ethanol so it precipitates\n• white / cloudy strands of DNA appear at the boundary between the layers\n• DNA can be lifted out / spooled on a glass rod\n• safety: ethanol is flammable, keep away from flames\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which are relevant. A few steps are given, e.g. mash the fruit and add ethanol, with little explanation of the substances used.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding, which is mostly relevant but may include gaps. Most steps are given in a sensible order, with the use of some substances explained and an expected observation.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. A logical, complete method in which the use of detergent, salt, water baths and ethanol is explained and the appearance of DNA is described.` },
      { q: `The Human Genome Project (HGP) sequenced the human genome. Today, a person can have their own genome sequenced for a few hundred pounds. Some people think that everyone should have their genome sequenced at birth.\n\n*Discuss the benefits and drawbacks of sequencing a person's genome.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nBenefits\n• genome is the entire DNA / all the genes of an organism\n• can identify alleles linked to inherited disorders, e.g. cystic fibrosis\n• can show an increased risk of diseases such as some cancers or heart disease\n• person can make lifestyle changes / have regular check-ups / early screening to reduce risk\n• earlier diagnosis and treatment\n• personalised medicine: the most effective drug / dose chosen for that person's alleles, fewer side effects\n• helps research into new treatments / gene therapy\n• couples can find out if they are carriers before having children\nDrawbacks\n• having an allele only increases risk, it does not mean the person will develop the disease (environment also matters)\n• may cause anxiety / stress about diseases that may never develop\n• there may be no treatment or cure for a disorder that is found\n• information could be used by insurance companies / employers to discriminate\n• privacy / data security concerns, information could be shared or stolen\n• also gives information about relatives, who have not consented\n• consent: a baby cannot consent to its genome being sequenced\n• cost to the health service\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which are relevant. One benefit or one drawback is given with little explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding, which is mostly relevant but may include gaps. Benefits and drawbacks are both given, with some explanation of at least one.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. A balanced discussion of benefits and drawbacks, each supported by explanation, e.g. linking alleles to risk of disease and to personalised medicine.` }
    ]
  },
  '4': {
    name: 'Natural Selection and Genetic Modification',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about human evolution.\nIn 1974, scientists in Ethiopia found a fossil skeleton of a female of the species Australopithecus afarensis. The fossil is known as Lucy.\n\nGive the approximate age of the Lucy fossil.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `3.2 million years (1)\nAccept any value from 3.0 to 3.5 million years.\nDo not accept 4.4 million years (Ardi) or 1.6 million years (Turkana Boy).` },
      { q: `This question is about classification.\nOrganisms used to be classified into five kingdoms. In 1977, a scientist studied the chemical sequences in the genetic material of many microorganisms. The results led to a new system of classification with three domains.\n\nName the scientist who proposed the three-domain system.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Carl Woese (1)\nAccept Woese.` },
      { q: `The forelimbs of a human, a bat, a whale and a mole look very different and are used for different purposes. Each forelimb contains one upper bone, two lower bones, a group of wrist bones and five digits.\n\nState what this similarity in limb structure suggests about these four mammals.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `they share a common ancestor / they evolved from the same ancestor (1)\nAccept they are related (through evolution).\nDo not accept 'they are the same species'.` },
      { q: `This question is about genetic engineering.\nScientists cut the gene for human insulin out of a human chromosome. The gene is then inserted into a bacterial plasmid.\n\nName the type of enzyme used to cut the insulin gene out of the human chromosome.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `restriction enzyme (1)\nAccept restriction endonuclease.\nDo not accept ligase.` },
      { q: `A plant nursery produces orchid plants using tissue culture. Small pieces of tissue are cut from a parent orchid and placed on agar jelly containing nutrients and plant hormones.\n\nGive one reason why the agar jelly must be sterile.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• so that microorganisms / bacteria / fungi do not grow on the agar (1)\n• so microorganisms do not compete with the plant tissue for nutrients (1)\n• so the plant tissue is not infected / killed by pathogens (1)` },
      { q: `A farmer grows tomatoes in a greenhouse. Aphids feed on the tomato plants and reduce the yield. The farmer releases ladybirds into the greenhouse. Ladybirds eat aphids.\n\nState one advantage of using ladybirds rather than a chemical pesticide to control the aphids.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `any one from:\n• no chemical residues left on the tomatoes / food (1)\n• does not harm other (useful) organisms / is specific to the pest (1)\n• aphids do not become resistant (1)\n• ladybirds reproduce so the control continues / does not need to be reapplied (1)\nAccept does not pollute the environment.` },
      { q: `This question is about evolution.\nCharles Darwin published his theory of evolution by natural selection in 1859, in the book 'On the Origin of Species'. Many people at the time did not accept his theory.\n\nGive two reasons why many people did not accept Darwin's theory in 1859.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• it conflicted with religious beliefs / the idea that God created all living things (1)\n• there was not enough evidence at the time / few fossils of intermediate forms had been found (1)\n• the mechanism of inheritance / genes / DNA was not known (1)\n• people did not accept that humans were related to / evolved from apes (1)` },
      { q: `Alfred Russel Wallace collected thousands of animal specimens in south-east Asia. He developed a theory of evolution by natural selection at the same time as Darwin. In 1858 Darwin and Wallace presented their ideas together.\n\nWallace also studied warning colouration in animals, such as brightly coloured caterpillars that taste unpleasant to birds.\n\nExplain how natural selection could lead to most caterpillars in a population having warning colouration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• brightly coloured caterpillars are less likely to be eaten (by birds) / birds learn to avoid them (1)\n• so they survive and reproduce / breed and pass on the allele(s) for bright colour (1)\nAccept genes for alleles.` },
      { q: `A student measured the beak depth of five medium ground finches on one of the Galápagos islands.\n\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question)\nBird: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nBeak depth in mm: 9.4, 10.1, 9.8, 10.6, 9.6\n\nCalculate the mean beak depth of these finches.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 9.4 + 10.1 + 9.8 + 10.6 + 9.6 = 49.5 (1)\n• 49.5 ÷ 5 = 9.9 (mm) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `MRSA is a strain of the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to several antibiotics. Table 2 shows the percentage of Staphylococcus aureus samples from hospital patients in one country that were MRSA.\n\nTable 2 (data written for this practice question)\nYear: 1996, 1999, 2002, 2006\nPercentage of samples that were MRSA (%): 8, 17, 29, 36\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the percentage of samples that were MRSA from 1996 to 2006.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (36 − 8) ÷ 8 × 100 / 28 ÷ 8 × 100 (1)\n• 350 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAward 1 mark for an answer of 3.5 or 450.` },
      { q: `A doctor prescribes a course of antibiotics to a patient with a bacterial infection. The doctor tells the patient to take all of the tablets, even if they start to feel better.\n\nExplain why the patient should finish the course of antibiotics.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• so that all of the bacteria are killed (1)\n• so there are no (partly) resistant bacteria left to survive and reproduce / spread (1)\nAccept reduces the chance of antibiotic-resistant strains developing for the second mark.` },
      { q: `This question is about human evolution.\nTable 3 shows information about three fossil hominids.\n\nTable 3 (approximate values)\nFossil: Ardi; Lucy; Turkana Boy\nSpecies: Ardipithecus ramidus; Australopithecus afarensis; Homo erectus\nAge in millions of years: 4.4; 3.2; 1.6\nBrain volume in cm³: 350; 450; 880\n\nDescribe the relationship between the age of the fossils and their brain volume, as shown in Table 3.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the more recent / younger the fossil, the larger the brain volume (1)\n• use of data, e.g. brain volume increased from 350 cm³ to 880 cm³ from 4.4 to 1.6 million years ago / the biggest increase was between Lucy and Turkana Boy (1)\nAccept the reverse argument: older fossils have smaller brains.` },
      { q: `This question is about human evolution.\nTable 3 shows information about three fossil hominids.\n\nTable 3 (approximate values)\nFossil: Ardi; Lucy; Turkana Boy\nAge in millions of years: 4.4; 3.2; 1.6\nBrain volume in cm³: 350; 450; 880\n\nCalculate how many times larger the brain volume of Turkana Boy is than the brain volume of Ardi.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 880 ÷ 350 (1)\n• 2.5 (times) (1)\nAward 1 mark for 2.51 or 2.514 (not given to 2 significant figures).` },
      { q: `Scientists found a stone hand axe at a site that also contained the remains of a wooden spear and a campfire.\n\nDescribe two methods that scientists could use to estimate the age of the hand axe.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• use the (age of the) layer of rock / sediment the tool was found in (stratigraphy) (1)\n• carbon dating of organic material found with the tool, e.g. the wooden spear / charcoal from the fire (1)\n• compare its complexity / shape with other tools of known age (1)\n• radiometric dating of volcanic rock / ash layers above or below the tool (1)` },
      { q: `The forelimbs of a human, a bat and a whale all have the same arrangement of bones, with five digits. The human uses its arm for grasping, the bat uses its wing for flying and the whale uses its flipper for swimming.\n\nExplain how the pentadactyl limb provides evidence for evolution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the limbs have a similar (basic) structure / same five-digit bone pattern (1)\n• suggesting the organisms evolved from a common ancestor, with the limb then adapted for different functions (1)` },
      { q: `Three organisms are described below.\n• Organism P: a bacterium found in the human gut. It has no nucleus.\n• Organism Q: a microorganism found in hot springs at 90 °C. It has no nucleus. Its genes and cell membranes are very different from those of bacteria.\n• Organism R: yeast. Its cells have a nucleus.\n\nComplete the table by giving the domain that each organism belongs to.\nOrganism: P, Q, R\nDomain: ………`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• P = Bacteria; Q = Archaea; R = Eukarya / Eukaryota\nAll three correct (2)\nOne or two correct (1)` },
      { q: `In the five-kingdom system, all organisms without a nucleus were placed in one kingdom, the prokaryotes. Later, genetic analysis showed that some of these prokaryotes have DNA sequences that are more similar to those of eukaryotes than to those of bacteria.\n\nExplain why genetic analysis led scientists to change from the five-kingdom system to the three-domain system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• genetic analysis / DNA / RNA sequencing showed that prokaryotes were two very different groups (1)\n• so prokaryotes were split into Archaea and Bacteria, with all other organisms in Eukarya (1)\nAccept classification based on genetic relatedness is more accurate than classification based on observable features.` },
      { q: `Pugs are a breed of dog that have been selectively bred for a very short, flat face. Many pugs have breathing problems. Pug breeders often breed closely related dogs together.\n\nExplain why selective breeding of closely related dogs increases the chance of the puppies having a genetic disorder.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• closely related dogs are more likely to carry the same (harmful) recessive alleles / there is reduced genetic variation (1)\n• so the puppies are more likely to inherit two copies of a harmful recessive allele (1)\nAccept inbreeding for selective breeding of closely related dogs.` },
      { q: `A grower wants to produce 5000 banana plants that all have high resistance to a fungal disease. The grower could grow plants from seeds, or use tissue culture of cells taken from one resistant plant.\n\nGive two advantages of using tissue culture rather than growing the plants from seeds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• all the plants are clones / genetically identical to the parent, so they will all be resistant to the disease (1)\n• large numbers of plants can be produced (from a small amount of tissue) (1)\n• plants can be produced quickly / all year round (1)\n• can be used for plants that do not produce seeds / are hard to grow from seed (1)\nAccept plants are produced in sterile conditions so are disease-free.` },
      { q: `Scientists grow human skin cells in tissue culture. The cells are kept in flasks containing a nutrient solution at 37 °C.\n\nGive two reasons why tissue culture of animal cells is useful in medical research.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• can test the effect of new drugs on cells / tissues (1)\n• fewer animals / humans needed for testing (1)\n• can study how cells grow / divide / specialise (1)\n• cells are genetically identical so results are comparable / it is a fair test (1)\n• can grow tissue for transplants / grafts, e.g. skin grafts for burns (1)` },
      { q: `In the production of human insulin by bacteria, scientists use the same restriction enzyme to cut the human DNA and to cut open the bacterial plasmid.\n\nExplain why the same restriction enzyme is used to cut both the human DNA and the plasmid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it produces the same / complementary sticky ends (on the gene and the plasmid) (1)\n• so the bases on the sticky ends can pair up / the gene can be joined into the plasmid (by ligase) (1)` },
      { q: `Bt cotton has been genetically modified to contain a gene from the bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis. This gene makes the plant produce a protein that kills insect pests.\n\nTable 4 shows the mean yield of cotton on farms in one region.\n\nTable 4 (data written for this practice question)\nType of cotton: non-Bt cotton; Bt cotton\nMean yield in kg per hectare: 1850; 2290\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean yield when Bt cotton is grown.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2290 − 1850) ÷ 1850 × 100 / 440 ÷ 1850 × 100 (1)\n• 23.8 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working.\nAward 1 mark for 23.78 or 24 (not to 1 decimal place).` },
      { q: `Some soya plants have been genetically modified to be resistant to a herbicide. Farmers spray their fields with the herbicide to kill weeds without killing the soya.\n\nExplain one possible risk to the environment of growing herbicide-resistant crops.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any one risk with a linked explanation:\n• pollen may transfer the gene to wild plants / weeds (1) producing herbicide-resistant 'superweeds' that are hard to control (1)\n• more herbicide may be used (1) which can harm / kill other plants / reduce biodiversity / pollute waterways (1)\n• fewer wild plants / weeds (1) so less food / habitat for insects / birds, reducing biodiversity (1)` },
      { q: `In some countries many children have a diet based mainly on rice. Rice contains very little beta-carotene. Beta-carotene is used by the body to make vitamin A. Golden rice is a genetically modified rice that produces beta-carotene in the rice grains.\n\nExplain how growing golden rice could improve the health of these children.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• golden rice provides beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A (1)\n• which reduces vitamin A deficiency / blindness / infections / deaths in children (1)` },
      { q: `A farmer added different masses of nitrate fertiliser to five fields of wheat. All the fields had the same soil.\n\nTable 5 (data written for this practice question)\nMass of nitrate added in kg per hectare: 0, 50, 100, 150, 200\nYield of wheat in tonnes per hectare: 4.2, 6.1, 7.6, 8.1, 8.2\n\nExplain why adding nitrate fertiliser increased the yield of wheat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• nitrates are needed to make amino acids / proteins (1)\n• so the plants can grow more / make more cells / grain (1)\nAccept nitrogen for nitrate.\nDo not accept 'nitrates give plants energy'.` },
      { q: `A farmer added different masses of nitrate fertiliser to five fields of wheat.\n\nTable 5 (data written for this practice question)\nMass of nitrate added in kg per hectare: 0, 50, 100, 150, 200\nYield of wheat in tonnes per hectare: 4.2, 6.1, 7.6, 8.1, 8.2\n\nThe farmer decides to add 150 kg per hectare of nitrate in future, not 200 kg per hectare.\n\nSuggest two reasons for the farmer's decision.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• the yield only increases by 0.1 tonnes per hectare from 150 to 200 kg per hectare / yield levels off (1)\n• extra fertiliser costs more money than the value of the extra wheat (1)\n• (excess) fertiliser may be washed / leached into rivers / cause eutrophication (1)\n• another factor is limiting growth (above 150 kg per hectare) (1)` },
      { q: `In 1935, cane toads were released in Australia to eat beetles that were damaging sugar cane crops. The cane toads did not eat many of the beetles. Cane toads are poisonous and have few predators in Australia. There are now over 200 million cane toads in Australia.\n\nExplain why the release of cane toads was a problem for other animals in Australia.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\n• the cane toad population increased rapidly because there were few predators (1)\n• cane toads compete with native species for food / eat native animals (1)\n• predators that eat the toads are poisoned / killed (1)\n• so native species decreased / biodiversity was reduced (1)` },
      { q: `Bacteria reproduce by binary fission. A single bacterium that is resistant to an antibiotic divides every 20 minutes.\n\nCalculate the number of resistant bacteria that would be produced from this one bacterium after 4 hours, if all the bacteria survive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4 hours = 240 minutes; 240 ÷ 20 = 12 divisions (1)\n• 2¹² = 4096 (bacteria) (1)\nAccept 4.1 × 10³.\nAward 1 mark for 2 × 12 = 24 only if 12 divisions is clearly stated.` },
      { q: `Medium ground finches on one Galápagos island feed on seeds. In 1977 there was a drought. Most of the plants that produced small, soft seeds died. Plants that produced large, hard seeds survived.\nScientists found that the mean beak depth of the finches that survived the drought was larger than before the drought, and the mean beak depth of their offspring was also larger.\n\nExplain why the mean beak depth of the finch population increased after the drought.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there was variation in beak depth (due to mutation / different alleles) (1)\n• finches with deeper beaks could crack the large, hard seeds so were more likely to survive the drought (1)\n• these finches reproduced / bred and passed on the alleles for deep beaks to their offspring (1)\nAccept genes for alleles.\nDo not accept 'finches grew bigger beaks to eat the seeds'.` },
      { q: `A patient in hospital has an infection caused by a population of bacteria. The patient is treated with an antibiotic. After several days the antibiotic no longer has an effect on the infection.\n\nExplain how the population of bacteria became resistant to the antibiotic.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a mutation produced a bacterium with resistance to the antibiotic (1)\n• the antibiotic killed the non-resistant bacteria, but the resistant bacteria survived (1)\n• the resistant bacteria reproduced / multiplied so the population of resistant bacteria increased (1)\nDo not accept 'the bacteria become immune' or 'the bacteria get used to the antibiotic'.` },
      { q: `A scientist wants to test whether a new antibiotic, Z, is effective against a strain of bacteria that is resistant to penicillin.\nThe scientist spreads the bacteria on an agar plate. Paper discs soaked in penicillin, antibiotic Z and sterile water are placed on the agar. The plate is incubated at 25 °C for 48 hours.\n\nTable 6 (data written for this practice question)\nDisc: penicillin; antibiotic Z; sterile water\nDiameter of clear zone in mm: 0; 18; 0\n\nExplain what these results show about antibiotic Z and the use of the sterile water disc.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• antibiotic Z kills / inhibits the growth of the bacteria (1)\n• because there is a clear zone of 18 mm around the Z disc but no clear zone around the penicillin disc (1)\n• the sterile water disc is a control, to show that the paper disc / water does not kill the bacteria (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about human evolution.\nThe fossils Ardi, Lucy and Turkana Boy were found in East Africa.\n• Ardi (4.4 million years old): brain volume about 350 cm³, long arms, big toe splayed to the side.\n• Lucy (3.2 million years old): brain volume about 450 cm³, big toe in line with other toes.\n• Turkana Boy (1.6 million years old): brain volume about 880 cm³, short arms and long legs, like a modern human.\n\nExplain how these fossils provide evidence that humans evolved from earlier hominids.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• brain volume increases as the fossils become more recent / younger (1)\n• the fossils become more like modern humans over time (1)\n• the feet / toes change from grasping to walking upright (1)\n• the arms get shorter and the legs longer / proportions become more like humans over time (1)\n• the fossils show a gradual change over millions of years (1)` },
      { q: `A wheat farmer has two varieties of wheat.\n• Variety A has a high yield but is easily damaged by a fungal disease.\n• Variety B has a low yield but is resistant to the fungal disease.\n\nDescribe how the farmer could use selective breeding to produce a variety of wheat with a high yield that is resistant to the fungal disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• cross-breed / cross-pollinate variety A with variety B (1)\n• select the offspring with high yield and resistance to the disease and breed them together (1)\n• repeat for many generations (until all offspring have both characteristics) (1)` },
      { q: `A garden centre wants to produce many new plants of a rare flowering plant. It uses tissue culture.\n\nDescribe how tissue culture is used to produce new plants.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• small pieces of tissue / explants are cut from the parent plant (e.g. from a shoot tip) (1)\n• the tissue is sterilised / aseptic technique is used (1)\n• the tissue is placed on agar / a growth medium containing nutrients (1)\n• plant hormones / auxins are added to stimulate growth of roots and shoots (1)\n• the small plants are transferred to compost / soil to grow (1)` },
      { q: `A farmer grows strawberries in a large polytunnel. Spider mites are damaging the strawberry plants. The farmer could spray the plants with a chemical pesticide or release predatory mites that eat spider mites.\n\nTable 7 (data written for this practice question)\nMethod: chemical pesticide; predatory mites\nCost per hectare: £180; £260\nTime to reduce spider mites by 90%: 2 days; 3 weeks\nNumber of applications needed in one season: 4; 1\n\nUsing the information in Table 7, evaluate the use of predatory mites rather than the chemical pesticide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• advantage: predatory mites need only 1 application / pesticide needs 4 applications so total pesticide cost is £720 compared with £260 (1)\n• disadvantage: predatory mites take longer to reduce the pests (3 weeks compared with 2 days) / cost more per application (1)\n• conclusion with reason, e.g. predatory mites are better because they are cheaper over the season and leave no chemical residues / do not harm other species / pests do not become resistant (1)\nAccept any other supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Some people are concerned about eating food from genetically modified (GM) crops.\nOther people say that GM crops are needed to feed the increasing human population.\n\nExplain two benefits and one risk of growing GM crops.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `benefits, any two from:\n• higher yield / more food produced (1)\n• resistance to insect pests, so less insecticide is needed (1)\n• resistance to herbicides, so weeds can be controlled more easily (1)\n• can grow in difficult conditions, e.g. drought (1)\n• increased nutritional value, e.g. golden rice (1)\nrisk, any one from:\n• genes may spread to wild plants, producing superweeds (1)\n• may reduce biodiversity / harm insects that are not pests (1)\n• possible long-term effects on human health are not fully known (1)\n• seeds are expensive for farmers to buy each year (1)` },
      { q: `People with type 1 diabetes need injections of insulin. Human insulin is now produced by genetically modified bacteria.\n\nDescribe how bacteria are genetically modified to produce human insulin.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the insulin gene is cut out of human DNA using a restriction enzyme (1)\n• a plasmid is cut open with the same restriction enzyme, producing sticky ends (1)\n• the insulin gene is joined into the plasmid using (DNA) ligase (1)\n• the recombinant plasmid / vector is inserted into a bacterium, which multiplies and produces insulin (1)\nAccept the bacteria are grown in a fermenter and the insulin is extracted / purified, for the fourth mark.` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out how the concentration of nitrate fertiliser affects the growth of radish seedlings.\n\nDevise a method the student could use.\nYou may use standard laboratory equipment and the materials listed: radish seeds, compost, plant pots, fertiliser solutions of different nitrate concentrations, distilled water.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `any four from:\n• grow seedlings in pots and water each pot with a different concentration of fertiliser solution (1)\n• use a range of at least 3 concentrations, with distilled water as a control (1)\n• control variables, e.g. same volume of solution / same volume of compost / same number of seeds / same light / temperature (1)\n• measure growth after a set time, e.g. height / mass of seedlings after 2 weeks (1)\n• repeat for each concentration and calculate a mean (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about human evolution.\nTable 8 shows information about fossil hominids and the stone tools found with them.\n\nTable 8 (approximate values)\nSpecies: Ardipithecus ramidus (Ardi); Australopithecus afarensis (Lucy); Homo habilis; Homo erectus (Turkana Boy); Homo sapiens\nApproximate age in millions of years: 4.4; 3.2; 2.4–1.4; 1.8–0.3; 0.2 to present\nBrain volume in cm³: 350; 450; 600; 880; 1350\nStone tools: none found; none found; simple pebble tools with a few flakes removed; tear-drop-shaped hand axes chipped on both sides; finely shaped blades, arrowheads and tools for scraping\n\n*Explain how the evidence from fossils and stone tools supports the theory that humans evolved over millions of years.\nYou should use information from Table 8 and your own knowledge in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nFossils\n• brain volume increased over time, from 350 cm³ (Ardi, 4.4 million years) to 1350 cm³ (Homo sapiens)\n• Ardi had a splayed big toe for climbing and long arms; Lucy's toes were in line, showing she walked upright\n• Turkana Boy had long legs and short arms, closer to modern human proportions\n• fossils can be dated by the rock layers they are found in; older fossils are in deeper layers\n• fossils show a gradual change in features, suggesting a common ancestor\nStone tools\n• no tools are found with the oldest hominids\n• tools become more complex / more finely shaped as the hominids become more recent\n• increased complexity links to larger brains / greater intelligence / skill\n• tools are dated by stratigraphy / carbon dating of material found with them\nLimitation\n• the fossil record is incomplete / few complete specimens have been found\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Uses data from both fossils and tools, linking changes in brain volume to tool complexity, to give a well-developed explanation with logical connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate biological understanding. Uses some data from fossils and tools to explain the evidence, with some logical connections.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Makes simple statements about fossils or tools with limited use of data.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `A government in a country with a growing population wants farmers to increase crop yields. Farmers have been offered three possible solutions:\n• growing a genetically modified (GM) variety of maize that is resistant to insect pests\n• using artificial fertilisers containing nitrates, phosphates and potassium\n• using biological control to reduce insect pests\n\n*Evaluate the benefits and risks of each of these solutions for increasing crop yields.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nGM maize\n• benefits: fewer crops lost to insects so higher yield; less insecticide sprayed, saving money and reducing harm to other species\n• risks: genes may spread to wild relatives; harmful insects may become resistant; possible harm to insects that are not pests; uncertain long-term effects on health; seeds may be expensive\nFertilisers\n• benefits: replace minerals removed by crops; nitrates for proteins / growth, phosphates and potassium for healthy growth; quick and reliable increase in yield\n• risks: cost; excess fertiliser leaches into rivers causing eutrophication / death of aquatic organisms; over-use beyond the point where yield levels off is wasteful\nBiological control\n• benefits: no chemical residues; specific to the pest; pests do not become resistant; long-lasting as the predator reproduces\n• risks: slower to act; the control organism may become a pest itself / eat other species / outcompete native species (e.g. cane toads); may not remove all pests\nConclusion\n• a reasoned judgement, e.g. a combination of solutions is best, or one solution is preferred with a justified reason\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Considers benefits and risks of all three solutions in a balanced way and reaches a judgement supported by the points made.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate biological understanding. Considers benefits and risks of at least two solutions; a judgement may be given but is only partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Gives simple statements about benefits or risks with little or no comparison or judgement.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '5': {
    name: 'Health, Disease and the Development of Medicines',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about health and disease.\n\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) gives a definition of health.\n\nComplete the sentence by putting the correct word in the space.\n\nHealth is a state of complete physical, mental and __________ well-being, and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `social (1)\ndo not accept emotional / economic` },
      { q: `Ash trees in the UK are being killed by a disease called chalara ash dieback. Infected trees lose their leaves and develop lesions in the bark.\n\nName the type of pathogen that causes chalara ash dieback.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `fungus (1)\naccept fungi / fungal\ndo not accept bacterium / virus / protist` },
      { q: `Malaria is caused by a protist called Plasmodium. The protist damages the blood and the liver of the infected person.\n\nThe protist is carried from one person to another by an animal vector.\n\nName the animal vector of malaria.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `mosquito (1)\naccept (female) Anopheles mosquito\nignore insect / fly unqualified` },
      { q: `The body has chemical barriers that help to prevent infection.\n\nTears contain an enzyme called lysozyme.\n\nState how lysozyme protects the eye from bacteria.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `lysozyme breaks down / digests bacterial cell walls (1)\naccept kills bacteria by damaging their cell walls\nignore kills bacteria unqualified\ndo not accept kills viruses` },
      { q: `Many medicines were first discovered in plants. Aspirin was first obtained from willow trees.\n\nThe heart drug digitalis was first obtained from another plant.\n\nName the plant from which digitalis was first obtained.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `foxglove (1)\naccept Digitalis purpurea` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of an antiseptic on the growth of bacteria on agar plates.\n\nAfter inoculating each plate, the student fixed the lid to the Petri dish with two short pieces of sticky tape. The student did not seal all the way around the lid.\n\nGive one reason why the student did not seal all the way around the lid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to allow oxygen into the dish (1)\naccept to stop anaerobic bacteria growing / to prevent harmful anaerobic pathogens growing\nignore to let air in unqualified\nignore to stop the plate being opened` },
      { q: `Cholera is a disease caused by the bacterium Vibrio cholerae. In 2010 there was a large cholera outbreak in Haiti after an earthquake damaged water supplies.\n\nExplain how providing treated drinking water reduces the spread of cholera.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `cholera (bacteria) is spread in (contaminated) water (1)\ntreatment / chlorination / boiling kills the bacteria (in the water) (1)\naccept so people do not drink water containing the pathogen\nignore germs\nignore cleans the water unqualified` },
      { q: `Tuberculosis (TB) is a disease caused by bacteria that damage the lungs. TB is spread through the air when infected people cough or sneeze.\n\nDescribe two ways the spread of TB can be reduced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• isolate / keep infected people away from others (1)\n• cover mouth and nose when coughing / sneezing (1)\n• treat infected people with antibiotics (for the full course) (1)\n• vaccinate people (against TB) (1)\n• improve ventilation / reduce overcrowding in housing (1)\nignore wash hands\nignore wear masks unless linked to infected person` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that infects the immune system. In some countries, many people with HIV also have tuberculosis (TB).\n\nExplain why a person with HIV is more likely to develop TB than a person without HIV.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `HIV destroys / damages white blood cells / lymphocytes (1)\naccept immune system is weakened\nso the body cannot fight / destroy the TB bacteria (as effectively) (1)\naccept fewer antibodies are produced against TB\nignore HIV causes TB` },
      { q: `In 2014 there was a large outbreak of Ebola in West Africa. Ebola is a communicable disease caused by a virus. It causes haemorrhagic fever.\n\n(i) State what is meant by a communicable disease.\n(ii) Give one way the spread of Ebola can be reduced.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `(i) a disease that can be passed / transmitted from one person / organism to another (1)\naccept a disease caused by a pathogen\nignore contagious unqualified\n(ii) any one from:\n• isolate infected people (1)\n• health workers wear protective clothing / gloves / masks (1)\n• avoid contact with body fluids of infected people (1)\n• safe burial of the dead / do not touch dead bodies (1)\n• screen people travelling from affected areas (1)\nignore vaccinate unqualified` },
      { q: `Chlamydia is a sexually transmitted infection (STI) caused by a bacterium. Many people with chlamydia have no symptoms.\n\nDescribe two ways the spread of chlamydia can be reduced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• use condoms (during sex) (1)\n• screening / testing (young) people for chlamydia (1)\n• treat infected people (and their partners) with antibiotics (1)\n• reduce the number of sexual partners / abstinence (1)\nignore contraceptive pill\nignore vaccination` },
      { q: `Chalara ash dieback was first found in the UK in 2012. Within a few years it had been found in ash trees across most of the UK.\n\nExplain why chalara ash dieback spread across the UK so quickly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the fungus produces spores (1)\nspores are carried (long distances) by the wind / in the air (1)\naccept infected (young) ash trees were imported / transported / moved between places (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `Insecticide-treated bed nets are given to people living in areas where malaria is common. Mosquitoes that carry malaria usually feed on human blood at night.\n\nExplain how sleeping under an insecticide-treated bed net reduces the spread of malaria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `prevents mosquitoes biting the person / feeding on their blood (1)\naccept insecticide kills the mosquitoes (that land on the net)\nso the protist / Plasmodium / pathogen is not transferred into (or out of) the blood (1)\nignore germs / virus / bacteria\ndo not accept mosquito causes malaria` },
      { q: `Plants have defences against pathogens. Some of these defences are physical and some are chemical.\n\nDescribe one physical defence and one chemical defence that plants use against pathogens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `physical, any one from:\n• (cellulose) cell wall (1)\n• waxy (leaf) cuticle (1)\n• bark / layer of dead cells on stems (1)\nchemical, any one from:\n• produce antiseptics / antibacterial chemicals (1)\n• produce poisons / toxins (to deter herbivores) (1)\nignore thorns / hairs (these deter animals, not pathogens) unless clearly linked to physical defence\nmax 1 for physical, max 1 for chemical` },
      { q: `A farmer noticed that some tomato plants in a greenhouse had yellow, spotted leaves. The farmer sent leaf samples to a laboratory to find out which pathogen was causing the disease.\n\nDescribe two ways that scientists in the laboratory could identify the pathogen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• DNA analysis / testing (of the pathogen) (1)\n• identifying antigens (of the pathogen) (1)\n• using monoclonal antibodies (that bind to the pathogen's antigens) (1)\n• growing / culturing the pathogen and looking at it with a microscope (1)\nignore looking at the symptoms / leaves (this is in the field)` },
      { q: `The trachea is lined with cells that produce mucus and cells that have cilia. Chemicals in tobacco smoke stop cilia from moving.\n\nExplain why smokers are more likely to get lung infections than non-smokers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mucus (and trapped pathogens) is not moved up / out of the trachea / airways (1)\nso pathogens reach / stay in the lungs (and cause infection) (1)\naccept more mucus builds up in the lungs, providing conditions for pathogens to grow\nignore dirt / dust unqualified` },
      { q: `The human body has physical and chemical barriers that stop pathogens entering the body.\n\nDescribe how the skin and the stomach each protect the body against pathogens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `skin: acts as a physical barrier / layer that pathogens cannot pass through (1)\naccept skin forms scabs / seals cuts\nstomach: (hydrochloric) acid kills pathogens (in food / drink) (1)\naccept low pH kills bacteria\nignore enzymes in the stomach` },
      { q: `A baby is too young to be given the measles vaccine. In the area where the baby lives, 96% of children have been vaccinated against measles.\n\nExplain how vaccinating most children in the area protects the baby from measles.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(most people are immune so) the measles virus is less likely to spread / fewer people are infected (1)\nso the baby is less likely to come into contact with an infected person (1)\naccept herd immunity for 1 mark if no explanation given\nignore the baby becomes immune` },
      { q: `Table 1 shows the number of children in a region who were offered a vaccine in one year.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nTable 1\nNumber of children offered the vaccine: 1 250 000\nNumber of children who received the vaccine: 1 140 000\n\nCalculate the percentage of children who did not receive the vaccine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 250 000 − 1 140 000 = 110 000 (1)\n(110 000 ÷ 1 250 000) × 100 = 8.8 (%) (1)\naccept 100 − 91.2 = 8.8 for 2 marks\naward 1 mark for 91.2 (%)\naward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Pregnancy test kits contain monoclonal antibodies. A pregnant woman produces a hormone called hCG, which is present in her urine.\n\nExplain why the monoclonal antibodies in the test bind to hCG but not to other substances in the urine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `monoclonal antibodies are all identical / specific to one antigen (1)\nthe antibody (binding site) is complementary (in shape) to hCG (only) (1)\naccept fits hCG / hCG is the antigen\ndo not accept same shape as hCG\ndo not accept active site` },
      { q: `Monoclonal antibodies are produced from hybridoma cells.\n\nDescribe how a hybridoma cell is produced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `a lymphocyte / white blood cell (that produces a specific antibody) is used (1)\nit is fused / combined with a tumour / cancer / myeloma cell (1)\naccept a mouse is injected with an antigen and its lymphocytes collected for the first mark\nignore stem cell` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of garlic extract on the growth of bacteria. The student placed a paper disc soaked in garlic extract on an agar plate covered in bacteria. After incubation, there was a circular clear zone around the disc.\n\nThe diameter of the clear zone was 14 mm.\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone.\nUse the equation: area = πr²\nUse π = 3.14 or the π button on your calculator.\nGive your answer in mm².`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `r = 14 ÷ 2 = 7 (mm) (1)\narea = π × 7² = 154 (mm²) (1)\naccept 153.9 / 153.94 / 153.86\naward 1 mark for 615 / 615.4 / 615.8 (diameter used as radius)\naward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of three different antibiotics on the growth of bacteria on agar plates.\n\nThe student was told to use aseptic techniques.\n\nDescribe two aseptic techniques the student should use when inoculating the agar plates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• heat / flame the inoculating loop (until red hot) before and after use (1)\n• let the loop cool before touching the bacteria (1)\n• use sterile / sterilised Petri dishes and agar (1)\n• lift the lid of the Petri dish only slightly / for as short a time as possible (1)\n• work near a (lit) Bunsen burner (to draw air upwards) (1)\n• disinfect / wipe the bench (with disinfectant) (1)\n• pass the neck of the culture bottle through a flame (1)\nignore wear gloves / goggles\nignore wash hands` },
      { q: `A pharmaceutical company has developed a new drug to lower blood pressure. The drug is tested in a double-blind clinical trial. Some patients are given the new drug and some are given a placebo.\n\nExplain why this trial is double-blind.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `neither the patients nor the doctors know who has the drug and who has the placebo (1)\nso the results are not biased / the doctors and patients do not influence the results (1)\naccept to avoid the placebo effect influencing results only if linked to patients not knowing\nignore to make it a fair test unqualified` },
      { q: `Body mass index (BMI) is used to judge whether a person is a healthy mass.\n\nBMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²\n\nA person with a BMI above 25 is classed as overweight. A woman is 1.68 m tall and has a mass of 81 kg.\n\nCalculate the mass the woman needs to lose so that her BMI is 25.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass at BMI 25 = 25 × 1.68² = 70.56 (kg) (1)\n81 − 70.56 = 10.4 (kg) (1)\naccept 10.44 for 2 marks if not rounded\naward 1 mark for 70.6 (kg)\naward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Waist-to-hip ratio can be used to show whether a person is carrying too much fat around the abdomen. For women, a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.85 shows an increased risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nA woman has a waist measurement of 84 cm and a hip measurement of 96 cm.\n\nCalculate the woman's waist-to-hip ratio and state whether she has an increased risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `84 ÷ 96 = 0.875 (1)\naccept 0.88 / 0.9\n(increased risk) because 0.875 is above 0.85 (1)\nallow ecf from an incorrect ratio\ndo not accept 96 ÷ 84 = 1.14 for the first mark, but allow ecf for the second mark` },
      { q: `Smoking tobacco increases the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nExplain how smoking increases the risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\n• nicotine increases heart rate / blood pressure (1)\n• (chemicals in smoke) damage the lining of the arteries / blood vessels (1)\n• leading to build-up of fatty deposits / plaque / narrowing of arteries (1)\n• carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood (so the heart works harder) (1)\n• increases risk of blood clots (1)\nignore tar\nignore lung cancer` },
      { q: `Table 2 shows the percentage of adults in one country who were obese in four different years.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nTable 2\nYear: 1995, 2005, 2015, 2022\nPercentage of adults who were obese (%): 16, 23, 27, 26\n\nDescribe the trend in the percentage of adults who were obese from 1995 to 2022.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `increased (from 1995) to 2015 (1)\nthen decreased slightly / levelled off (from 2015) to 2022 (1)\naccept use of data, e.g. increased from 16% to 27% then fell to 26%\nignore increased unqualified for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Stomach ulcers can be caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori. Ebola is caused by a virus.\n\nExplain why antibiotics can be used to treat stomach ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori but cannot be used to treat Ebola.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `antibiotics kill / inhibit (the growth of) bacteria (1)\nby affecting processes / structures in the bacterial cell (e.g. cell wall) that are not in human cells (1)\nviruses are not cells / do not have these structures / reproduce inside host cells (1)\nso antibiotics do not affect viruses (without damaging the body's own cells) (1)\nmax 3\nignore viruses are immune to antibiotics` },
      { q: `Some viruses infect bacteria. These viruses can reproduce by the lytic pathway.\n\nDescribe the stages of the lytic pathway after the virus attaches to the bacterial cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• the virus injects its genetic material / DNA / RNA into the cell (1)\n• the viral genetic material is replicated / copied (using the cell's enzymes / materials) (1)\n• new virus particles / proteins are made / assembled in the cell (1)\n• the cell bursts / lyses, releasing the new viruses (1)\ndo not accept genetic material inserted into the host DNA (this is the lysogenic pathway)` },
      { q: `A person had a vaccination against a virus. A few months later, the person was infected with the same virus. Scientists measured the concentration of antibodies in the person's blood after the vaccination and after the infection.\n\nAfter the infection, antibodies were produced faster and in a much higher concentration than after the vaccination. The person did not become ill.\n\nExplain why the person did not become ill after the infection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `vaccination led to the production of memory lymphocytes / memory cells (1)\nmemory lymphocytes recognise the antigens on the virus (1)\nso antibodies are produced more quickly / in larger amounts (secondary response) (1)\nthe virus is destroyed before it can cause symptoms (1)\nmax 3\nignore white blood cells unqualified for memory cells\ndo not accept antibodies remain in the blood for the first mark` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that is spread through body fluids. If untreated, a person infected with HIV may develop AIDS. People with AIDS often die from infections that do not usually cause serious illness in healthy people.\n\nExplain why people with AIDS die from infections that do not usually cause serious illness.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `HIV infects / destroys lymphocytes / white blood cells (1)\nfewer antibodies are produced (1)\nso the immune system cannot destroy pathogens / fight infections (1)\naccept the person cannot develop immunity / memory cells\nignore HIV kills the person` },
      { q: `Scientists have discovered a new chemical that may be useful as a medicine. Before the medicine can be sold, it must go through several stages of development and testing.\n\nDescribe the stages of testing a new medicine before it can be given to patients by doctors.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• preclinical testing on cells / tissues (in the laboratory) (1)\n• (preclinical) testing on animals (1)\n• (clinical) testing on healthy volunteers (in low doses) to check it is safe / for side effects (1)\n• (clinical) testing on patients (with the disease) to check it is effective / to find the best dose (1)\n• (double-blind) trial with a placebo / existing drug (1)\naccept testing for toxicity / efficacy / dosage linked to an appropriate stage\nmax 3 if stages are in the wrong order` },
      { q: `Drinking large amounts of alcohol over many years can damage the liver.\n\nExplain why drinking large amounts of alcohol over many years can lead to liver disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `alcohol is a toxin / poison broken down by the liver (1)\nliver cells are damaged / killed (1)\nscar tissue / fibrous tissue forms / cirrhosis develops (1)\naccept liver cannot carry out its functions (e.g. remove toxins) / liver failure\naccept increased risk of liver cancer\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of a plant extract on the growth of bacteria. The student repeated the experiment three times. The diameters of the clear zones were 12 mm, 15 mm and 13 mm.\n\nCalculate the mean area of the clear zone.\nUse the equation: area = πr²\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean diameter = (12 + 15 + 13) ÷ 3 = 13.3 (mm) (1)\nradius = 13.3 ÷ 2 = 6.67 (mm) (1)\narea = π × 6.67² = 140 (mm²) (1)\naccept 139 / 139.6 / 138.9 (from rounded radius)\naccept answers calculated from the mean of the three individual areas: 141 mm²\naward full marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Statins are a type of medication given to people with a high risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nExplain how statins reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease and give one disadvantage of taking statins.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `statins reduce (LDL / blood) cholesterol (levels) (1)\nreducing the build-up of fatty deposits / plaque in arteries (1)\none disadvantage from:\n• side effects, e.g. muscle pain / liver damage / digestive problems (1)\n• must be taken for a long time / for life (1)\n• do not work immediately (1)\nignore expensive` },
      { q: `A student wanted to compare the effectiveness of three different antiseptics against bacteria.\n\nThe student was given:\n• agar plates\n• a culture of harmless bacteria\n• paper discs\n• solutions of three antiseptics\n• an incubator\n\nDevise a method the student could use to compare the effectiveness of the three antiseptics.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `any four from:\n• spread bacteria evenly on the agar plate (using aseptic technique / sterile spreader) (1)\n• soak paper discs in each antiseptic (for the same time) (1)\n• include a control disc soaked in (sterile) water (1)\n• place the discs on the agar, evenly spaced (1)\n• tape the lid and incubate at 25 °C (for the same time, e.g. 48 hours) (1)\n• measure the diameter of the clear zone and calculate area using πr² (1)\n• the largest clear zone shows the most effective antiseptic (1)\n• repeat and calculate a mean (1)\nignore incubate at 37 °C\nmax 4` },
      { q: `Table 3 shows information about two men.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nTable 3\nMan A: mass 95 kg, height 1.80 m, waist 104 cm, hip 100 cm\nMan B: mass 102 kg, height 1.90 m, waist 86 cm, hip 101 cm, BMI 28.3\n\nBMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²\n\nA BMI of 25 to 29.9 is classed as overweight. For men, a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.9 shows an increased risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nMan B is a rugby player who trains every day.\n\nUse the information to explain which man is more at risk of cardiovascular disease.\nYou should include calculations in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `BMI of man A = 95 ÷ 1.80² = 29.3 (1)\nwaist-to-hip ratio of man A = 104 ÷ 100 = 1.04 and of man B = 86 ÷ 101 = 0.85 (1)\nman A is more at risk because his waist-to-hip ratio is above 0.9 / he has more fat around his abdomen (1)\nboth men have similar BMIs / are classed as overweight, but man B's high mass is likely to be muscle (so BMI is misleading for man B) (1)\naccept 0.851 / 0.8515 for man B` },
      { q: `*Measles is a disease caused by a virus. Children in the UK are offered a vaccine against measles.\n\nThe vaccine contains an inactive form of the measles virus.\n\nExplain how the vaccine protects a child from becoming ill with measles, and how the body responds if the child is later infected with the measles virus.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• the vaccine contains antigens (of the measles virus)\n• the inactive virus cannot cause the disease\n• lymphocytes / white blood cells with complementary antibodies recognise the antigen\n• these lymphocytes divide / are cloned\n• and produce antibodies specific to the measles antigen\n• antibodies bind to antigens / cause viruses to clump / help destroy the virus\n• memory lymphocytes are produced and remain in the blood\n• on later infection, memory lymphocytes recognise the antigen\n• secondary response: antibodies produced faster / in greater quantity\n• virus destroyed before symptoms develop / child is immune\n• if most children are vaccinated, herd immunity reduces spread\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate. Understanding of scientific ideas lacks detail. Presents an explanation with some structure and coherence.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant but may include some inaccuracies. Understanding of scientific ideas is not fully detailed and/or developed. Presents an explanation that has a structure which is mostly clear, coherent and logical, e.g. describes antibody production after vaccination and links memory cells to protection.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Understanding of the scientific ideas is detailed and fully developed. Presents an explanation that has a well-developed structure which is clear, coherent and logical, linking antigens, specific antibodies, memory lymphocytes and a faster, larger secondary response.\n0 marks: no rewardable material.` },
      { q: `*Coronary heart disease is a cardiovascular disease. Fatty deposits build up in the coronary arteries, which narrows them and reduces blood flow to the heart muscle.\n\nTreatments for cardiovascular disease include:\n• lifestyle changes\n• medication\n• surgical procedures.\n\nDiscuss the benefits and risks of these treatments for cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nlifestyle changes\n• e.g. stop smoking, healthier diet / less saturated fat, more exercise, lose weight, reduce alcohol\n• benefits: no side effects / cheap / no surgery / reduces risk of other diseases\n• risks: slow to take effect / may not be enough for severe disease / relies on the person sticking to it\nmedication\n• statins reduce cholesterol, anticoagulants / aspirin reduce blood clotting, antihypertensives reduce blood pressure\n• benefits: no surgery needed / effective at reducing risk of heart attack / stroke\n• risks: side effects, e.g. muscle pain, bleeding (anticoagulants), liver damage; must be taken long term\nsurgical procedures\n• stents keep arteries open; bypass surgery uses a blood vessel to carry blood around a blockage; heart transplant\n• benefits: immediate / long-lasting improvement in blood flow\n• risks: infection, blood clots, bleeding, reaction to anaesthetic, rejection of transplant (needing immunosuppressants), shortage of donors, longer recovery time\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate. Understanding of scientific ideas lacks detail. Presents a discussion with some structure and coherence.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant but may include some inaccuracies. Presents a discussion of benefits and risks of at least two types of treatment, with a structure that is mostly clear, coherent and logical.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Presents a balanced discussion of benefits and risks of all three types of treatment, with a well-developed structure which is clear, coherent and logical.\n0 marks: no rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '6': {
    name: 'Plant Structures and Their Functions',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student investigated photosynthesis in pondweed. The pondweed was placed in a boiling tube of water near a lamp. Bubbles of gas were given off from the cut end of the stem.\n\nName the gas in the bubbles.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `oxygen / O₂ (1)\nDo not accept carbon dioxide.` },
      { q: `This question is about photosynthesis.\nPhotosynthesis is an endothermic reaction.\n\nState what is meant by an endothermic reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `a reaction that takes in (heat / light) energy from the surroundings (1)\nAccept absorbs energy / energy is taken in.\nDo not accept a reaction that releases energy.` },
      { q: `This question is about transport in plants.\nRoot hair cells take in water from the soil. The soil water is a more dilute solution than the cytoplasm of the root hair cell.\n\nName the process by which water moves into the root hair cell.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `osmosis (1)\nDo not accept diffusion or active transport.` },
      { q: `Aphids are small insects that feed on plants. An aphid pushes its mouthparts into the phloem of a stem and feeds on the liquid inside.\n\nName the sugar that is transported in the phloem.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `sucrose (1)\nDo not accept glucose / starch.` },
      { q: `Bananas are picked from the plants while they are still green and unripe. They are transported to the UK in containers. Before the bananas are sold, the containers are filled with a plant hormone that makes the bananas ripen.\n\nName this plant hormone.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `ethene (1)\nAccept ethylene.\nDo not accept auxin / gibberellin.` },
      { q: `A student set up a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. The student cut the stem of the shoot under water and fitted it into the potometer under water.\n\nGive one reason why the stem was cut and fitted under water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to stop air (bubbles) entering the xylem (1)\nAccept so that air bubbles do not block the xylem / so the water column is not broken / to stop air leaking into the apparatus.` },
      { q: `This question is about photosynthesis.\nAlgae in a pond use light energy to make glucose.\n\nComplete the word equation for photosynthesis.\n\n........................ + water → glucose + ........................`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `carbon dioxide (1)\noxygen (1)\nAccept CO₂ and O₂.\nThe answers must be in the correct places.` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed. The student placed a lamp at different distances from a boiling tube containing the pondweed and counted the bubbles given off. A beaker of water was placed between the lamp and the boiling tube.\n\nExplain why the beaker of water was placed between the lamp and the boiling tube.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `to absorb heat (energy) from the lamp (1)\nso that temperature is kept constant / temperature does not affect the rate of photosynthesis (1)\nAccept acts as a heat shield / heat filter for the first mark.\nAccept so that only light intensity affects the rate for the second mark.` },
      { q: `A student investigated photosynthesis in pondweed with a lamp 20 cm away. The student counted the bubbles given off in 5 minutes. The investigation was repeated three times (data written for this practice question).\nRepeat 1: 38 bubbles\nRepeat 2: 42 bubbles\nRepeat 3: 40 bubbles\n\nCalculate the mean rate of bubble production in bubbles per minute.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean number of bubbles = (38 + 42 + 40) ÷ 3 = 40 (1)\nrate = 40 ÷ 5 = 8 (bubbles per minute) (1)\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.\nAward 1 mark for 24 (bubbles per minute) (total ÷ 5 without finding the mean).` },
      { q: `A student placed a lamp 0.25 m from some pondweed.\nThe relative light intensity can be calculated using the equation\nrelative light intensity = 1 ÷ d²\nwhere d is the distance from the lamp in metres.\n\nCalculate the relative light intensity at a distance of 0.25 m.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.25² = 0.0625 / substitution 1 ÷ 0.25² (1)\nevaluation: 16 (arbitrary units) (1)\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.\nAward 1 mark for 4 (1 ÷ 0.25, distance not squared).` },
      { q: `A student investigating photosynthesis moved a lamp from 15 cm to 45 cm away from some pondweed.\nLight intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the light source.\n\nCalculate how many times lower the light intensity is at 45 cm than at 15 cm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `distance increased 3 times / 45 ÷ 15 = 3 (1)\nlight intensity is 3² = 9 times lower (1)\nAccept (1 ÷ 15²) ÷ (1 ÷ 45²) = 9.\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.\nAward 1 mark for 3 (times lower) only.` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different light intensities. The temperature was kept at 25 °C and the carbon dioxide concentration at 0.04%.\nThe rate increased as light intensity increased from 0 to 400 arbitrary units. Above 400 arbitrary units the rate stayed the same (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain why the rate of photosynthesis stayed the same above 400 arbitrary units of light intensity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `light intensity is no longer the limiting factor (1)\nanother factor such as carbon dioxide concentration / temperature is now limiting (1)\nAccept increasing light has no further effect because something else is in short supply for the first mark.` },
      { q: `This question is about photosynthesis.\nA student measured the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed at different temperatures. The rate increased up to 35 °C. At 45 °C the rate was much lower than at 35 °C.\n\nExplain why the rate of photosynthesis was lower at 45 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `enzymes (that control photosynthesis) are denatured (1)\nactive site changes shape so the substrate no longer fits / fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form (1)\nDo not accept enzymes are killed.` },
      { q: `This question is about plant cells.\nA root hair cell is a cell in the root of a plant. It has a long, thin extension that grows out between the soil particles.\n\nExplain how this extension helps the root hair cell to absorb water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `gives a large surface area (1)\nso more water can be absorbed by osmosis / water is absorbed faster (1)\nAccept reaches further between soil particles / into more soil water for the second mark.` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the concentration of nitrate ions in soil water and inside the root hair cells of a wheat plant (data written for this practice question).\nConcentration in soil water: 0.2 mmol per dm³\nConcentration inside the root hair cells: 5.0 mmol per dm³\n\nExplain why the root hair cells must use active transport to take in nitrate ions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `nitrate ions are moved against the concentration gradient / from a low concentration (in soil) to a high concentration (in the cell) (1)\nwhich needs energy from respiration / which diffusion cannot do (1)\nAccept ATP for energy.` },
      { q: `This question is about transport in plants.\nXylem vessels carry water from the roots to the leaves. Xylem is made of dead cells. The cell walls contain lignin, and there are no end walls between the cells.\n\nExplain how this structure is adapted for the function of xylem.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\nlignin strengthens the walls / stops the vessel collapsing / supports the plant (1)\nno end walls / hollow / no cytoplasm so water can flow in a continuous column (1)\nlignin makes the walls waterproof so water stays in the vessel (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `Phloem tissue is made of sieve tube cells and companion cells. Sieve tube cells have very few organelles. Companion cells contain many mitochondria.\n\nExplain why companion cells contain many mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mitochondria are the site of (aerobic) respiration / release energy (1)\nenergy is needed for translocation / to load sucrose into the sieve tubes / for active transport of sucrose (1)\nDo not accept make energy.` },
      { q: `A gardener noticed that aphids feeding on the stem of a bean plant grew more slowly when the plant was kept in the dark. Aphids feed on the liquid in the phloem.\n\nDescribe how sucrose is transported in a plant by translocation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\nsucrose is transported in the phloem / sieve tubes (1)\nfrom where it is made (in the leaves) to where it is used or stored (eg roots / growing tips / fruits) (1)\ncan move both up and down the plant (1)\nrequires energy / is an active process (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `This question is about stomata.\nA student looked at the lower surface of a leaf using a microscope. Each stoma was surrounded by two guard cells. The student saw that the stomata were open in the light and closed in the dark.\n\nExplain why closing the stomata in the dark is an advantage to the plant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reduces water loss / transpiration (1)\nbecause (there is no light so) photosynthesis is not taking place so carbon dioxide does not need to enter the leaf (1)\nAccept reduces wilting for the first mark.` },
      { q: `A student used a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. An air bubble in the capillary tube moved 36 mm in 4 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of water uptake in mm per minute.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `36 ÷ 4 (1)\nevaluation: 9 (mm per minute) (1)\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.` },
      { q: `A student used a potometer to investigate the effect of air movement on the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. When an electric fan was switched on near the shoot, the rate of water uptake increased.\n\nExplain why moving air increases the rate of water uptake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water vapour is moved away from the leaf (surface / stomata) (1)\nso a steeper concentration gradient (of water vapour) is kept and more water evaporates / diffuses out of the leaf (1)\nAccept faster transpiration so more water is drawn up the xylem for the second mark.` },
      { q: `This question is about transpiration.\nA gardener noticed that plants in a greenhouse needed more watering on hot days than on cool days.\n\nExplain why an increase in temperature increases the rate of transpiration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `water molecules have more kinetic energy / move faster (1)\nso water evaporates (from the mesophyll cells) and diffuses out of the stomata faster (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about leaf structure.\nThe spongy mesophyll layer of a leaf contains many air spaces between the cells.\n\nExplain how the air spaces help the leaf to carry out photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `allow carbon dioxide to diffuse (from the stomata) to the (palisade / mesophyll) cells (1)\nlarge surface area (of cells) in contact with air for gas exchange (1)\nAccept allow oxygen to diffuse out of the leaf.` },
      { q: `Cacti grow in hot deserts. A cactus has leaves that are thin spines and a thick, swollen green stem.\n\nExplain how having leaves that are spines helps a cactus to survive in a hot desert.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `smaller surface area (for evaporation) / fewer stomata (1)\nso less water is lost by transpiration (1)\nAccept spines protect the stem from animals that would eat it for water, max 1.` },
      { q: `A farmer sprays a selective weedkiller on a field of wheat. The weedkiller contains a synthetic auxin. Wheat is a narrow-leaved plant. The weeds in the field, such as dandelions, are broad-leaved plants.\n\nExplain how the weedkiller kills the weeds but not the wheat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `broad-leaved plants absorb more of the auxin / are more sensitive to the auxin (than narrow-leaved plants) (1)\nthe auxin causes (rapid) uncontrolled growth so the weeds die (1)\nAccept the wheat absorbs little auxin so is not affected for the first mark.` },
      { q: `Gibberellins are plant hormones. A fruit grower sprays gibberellins on grape plants so that the grapes grow larger. Brewers soak barley seeds in gibberellins.\n\nExplain why brewers add gibberellins to barley seeds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `gibberellins cause seeds to germinate / end seed dormancy (1)\nso that all the seeds germinate at the same time / so starch is broken down to sugars (for brewing) (1)\nAccept germination happens faster for the second mark.` },
      { q: `A gardener takes cuttings from a geranium plant. She dips the cut end of each stem into rooting powder before planting it in compost. Rooting powder contains auxin.\n\nExplain why the gardener uses rooting powder.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `auxin stimulates the growth of roots (at the cut end) (1)\nso the cutting grows roots quickly / more cuttings survive / produces many identical plants (1)` },
      { q: `Some plants grow in deserts where there is very little rain. Some desert plants have very long roots. Other desert plants have shallow roots that spread out widely just below the surface.\n\nExplain how each of these root adaptations helps a plant to survive where there is little water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `long roots reach water deep underground (1)\nshallow, widespread roots absorb rainwater quickly over a large area before it drains away / evaporates (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about increasing crop yields.\nA farmer grows tomatoes in a greenhouse. In winter the farmer burns paraffin in heaters inside the greenhouse. Burning paraffin releases heat and carbon dioxide.\n\nExplain how using the paraffin heaters increases the yield of tomatoes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `higher temperature increases the rate of photosynthesis / enzymes work faster (1)\nhigher carbon dioxide concentration increases the rate of photosynthesis (1)\ntemperature / carbon dioxide were limiting factors (in winter) (1)\nmore glucose produced for growth / making starch / cellulose / proteins (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A student used a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. The capillary tube had a radius of 0.5 mm. In 5 minutes the air bubble moved 24 mm along the tube.\n\nCalculate the volume of water taken up per minute.\nUse the equation\nvolume = π × r² × l\nGive your answer in mm³ per minute to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `volume = π × 0.5² × 24 (1)\n= 18.8(5) (mm³) (1)\nrate = 18.85 ÷ 5 = 3.77 (mm³ per minute) (1)\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.\nAward 2 marks for 18.8 / 18.9 mm³ (not divided by 5).\nAward 1 mark for 7.54 (mm³ per minute) (r not squared: π × 0.5 × 24 ÷ 5) or 4.8 (mm per minute).` },
      { q: `Some oat seedlings were grown in a box. Light could only enter the box through a hole on one side. After two days the shoots had all grown towards the hole.\n\nExplain how auxin causes the shoots to grow towards the light.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `auxin is made in the shoot tip (1)\nauxin moves to the shaded side of the shoot (1)\nauxin causes cell elongation / cells grow longer on the shaded side (1)\nso the shoot bends towards the light (positive phototropism) (1)\nmax 3\nDo not accept auxin moves towards the light.` },
      { q: `A student germinated some bean seeds. When the roots were 2 cm long, the student placed one seedling on its side so that its root was horizontal. After 24 hours the tip of the root had grown downwards.\n\nExplain how auxin causes the root to grow downwards.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `auxin collects on the lower side of the root (due to gravity) (1)\nin roots, auxin inhibits / slows cell elongation (1)\nso cells on the upper side grow longer (than the cells on the lower side) and the root bends downwards (positive gravitropism) (1)\nAccept geotropism for gravitropism.` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different light intensities at two carbon dioxide concentrations. The temperature was kept at 25 °C (data written for this practice question).\nLight intensity (arbitrary units): 1, 2, 4, 6, 8\nRate at 0.04% CO₂ (arbitrary units): 10, 20, 32, 34, 34\nRate at 0.10% CO₂ (arbitrary units): 10, 20, 38, 52, 60\n\nExplain the effect of carbon dioxide concentration on the rate of photosynthesis shown by these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `at low light intensity (1–2 units) the rate is the same at both concentrations because light intensity is the limiting factor (1)\nat 0.04% CO₂ the rate levels off at 34 (above 6 units) because carbon dioxide (concentration) is the limiting factor (1)\nat 0.10% CO₂ the rate is higher / keeps rising up to 60 because more carbon dioxide is available, so light is still limiting (1)\nAccept use of figures to support the comparison eg 60 compared with 34 at 8 units.` },
      { q: `Ripe fruits release ethene. A supermarket stores unripe bananas at a low temperature in a room with very little oxygen. A few days before the bananas are to be sold, they are moved to a warm room and ethene gas is added.\n\nExplain why the supermarket stores and then treats the bananas in this way.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `storing unripe bananas (cold / low oxygen) slows ripening / slows ethene production (1)\nso bananas do not ripen and spoil (become overripe) during storage / transport (1)\nadding ethene makes the bananas ripen (at the same time) so they are ripe when sold (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about transport in plants.\nWater moves through a plant in xylem vessels. Sucrose moves through a plant in phloem sieve tubes.\n\nCompare the structure of xylem vessels with the structure of phloem sieve tubes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any three from:\nxylem is made of dead cells but phloem is made of living cells (1)\nxylem walls are strengthened with lignin but phloem walls are not (1)\nxylem has no end walls but phloem has end walls with pores / sieve plates (1)\nxylem has no cytoplasm but sieve tubes contain some cytoplasm (1)\nphloem sieve tubes have companion cells but xylem does not (1)\nmax 3\nEach point must be a comparison.` },
      { q: `A student painted clear nail varnish on the lower surface of a leaf. When the varnish was dry, the student peeled it off and looked at it with a microscope. The student counted 18 stomata in the field of view. The area of the field of view was 0.12 mm².\n\n(i) Calculate the number of stomata per mm² of leaf.\n(ii) Suggest why there are more stomata on the lower surface of a leaf than on the upper surface.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i) 18 ÷ 0.12 (1)\n150 (stomata per mm²) (1)\nAward full marks for the correct answer without working.\n(ii) the lower surface is cooler / shaded / not in direct sunlight so less water is lost by evaporation / transpiration (1)` },
      { q: `A student used a potometer to investigate the effect of air movement on the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. The student measured how far the air bubble moved in 6 minutes, first in still air and then with a fan switched on (data written for this practice question).\nStill air: bubble moved 12 mm in 6 minutes\nFan switched on: bubble moved 39 mm in 6 minutes\n\n(i) Calculate the rate of water uptake in still air and with the fan switched on, in mm per minute.\n(ii) Calculate the percentage increase in the rate of water uptake when the fan was switched on.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i) still air: 12 ÷ 6 = 2(.0) (mm per minute) (1)\nfan: 39 ÷ 6 = 6.5 (mm per minute) (1)\n(ii) (6.5 − 2.0) ÷ 2.0 / 4.5 ÷ 2.0 (1)\n× 100 = 225 (%) (1)\nAllow ecf from (i).\nAward 1 mark in (ii) for 325% (6.5 ÷ 2.0 × 100) or 69% (4.5 ÷ 6.5 × 100).` },
      { q: `This question is about leaf structure.\nA leaf has a waxy cuticle, an upper epidermis, a palisade mesophyll layer, a spongy mesophyll layer and a lower epidermis with stomata. A leaf is usually broad and thin.\n\nExplain how the structure of a leaf is adapted for photosynthesis.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any four from:\nbroad / large surface area to absorb more light (1)\nthin so carbon dioxide has a short distance to diffuse (to the cells) (1)\nupper epidermis / cuticle is transparent so light passes through to the palisade layer (1)\npalisade cells are near the top surface / contain many chloroplasts to absorb more light (1)\nair spaces in the spongy mesophyll allow carbon dioxide to diffuse to the cells (1)\nstomata allow carbon dioxide to enter / oxygen to leave (1)\nxylem (in veins) brings water for photosynthesis / phloem removes sucrose (1)\nmax 4` },
      { q: `Algal balls are small balls of algae held together in a jelly. When the algae photosynthesise, they take in carbon dioxide. Hydrogencarbonate indicator changes colour from orange to purple as the carbon dioxide concentration of the solution decreases.\n\n*Devise a method that a student could use to investigate the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis using algal balls and hydrogencarbonate indicator.\nInclude in your answer:\n• how light intensity would be changed\n• how the rate of photosynthesis would be measured\n• the variables that would be controlled.`, marks: 6, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• put the same number of algal balls into each of several bottles / tubes\n• add the same volume of hydrogencarbonate indicator to each\n• place a lamp at different distances from the bottles eg 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 cm\n• measure each distance with a metre rule\n• use one bottle covered in foil / kept in the dark as a control\n• time how long it takes for the indicator to change to purple / record the colour after a fixed time\n• compare the colour with a colour chart / use a colorimeter\n• rate = 1 ÷ time taken\n• keep temperature constant eg use a water bath / heat shield / LED lamp\n• use the same type and power of lamp in a darkened room\n• keep the starting concentration of carbon dioxide / volume of indicator the same\n• repeat at each distance and calculate a mean\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate. Understanding of scientific ideas, processes, techniques and procedures lacks detail. Presents a method with some structure and coherence, eg changes distance of the lamp and looks at the colour.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding, which is mostly relevant but may include some inaccuracies. Understanding of scientific ideas, processes, techniques and procedures is not fully detailed and/or developed. Presents a method that has a structure which is mostly clear, coherent and logical, eg changes distance, measures time for colour change, controls one variable.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Understanding of the scientific ideas, processes, techniques and procedures is detailed and fully developed. Presents a method that has a well-developed structure which is clear, coherent and logical, covering how light intensity is changed, how rate is measured and at least two controlled variables.` },
      { q: `An oak tree can be more than 30 m tall. Each day in summer, a large oak tree can take in more than 100 litres of water from the soil. Most of this water is lost from the leaves.\n\n*Explain how water and mineral ions are taken in by the roots and transported to the leaves of the tree, and how the water is lost from the leaves.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• root hair cells have a large surface area for absorption\n• water enters root hair cells by osmosis\n• from a dilute solution in the soil to a more concentrated solution in the cell\n• mineral ions enter by active transport against the concentration gradient\n• active transport needs energy from respiration (mitochondria in root hair cells)\n• water moves across the root to the xylem\n• xylem vessels are hollow, dead cells with no end walls, forming continuous tubes\n• lignin strengthens xylem so it does not collapse\n• mineral ions are carried dissolved in the water in the xylem\n• water evaporates from mesophyll cells into the air spaces in the leaf\n• water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata\n• guard cells open the stomata in the light\n• loss of water from the leaves pulls water up the xylem in a continuous column (transpiration stream)\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): No rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate. Understanding of scientific ideas lacks detail. Presents an explanation with some structure and coherence, eg water enters the roots and goes up the xylem to the leaves.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates biological understanding, which is mostly relevant but may include some inaccuracies. Understanding of scientific ideas is not fully detailed and/or developed. Presents an explanation that has a structure which is mostly clear, coherent and logical, eg osmosis into root hair cells, transport in xylem and evaporation through stomata.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout. Understanding of the scientific ideas is detailed and fully developed. Presents an explanation that has a well-developed structure which is clear, coherent and logical, linking uptake (osmosis and active transport), xylem structure and transpiration as the force that pulls water up the tree.` }
    ]
  },
  '7': {
    name: 'Animal Coordination, Control and Homeostasis',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about hormones.\n\nA doctor examines a patient who has a swelling at the front of the neck. Blood tests show that the patient has a low concentration of the hormone thyroxine.\n\nName the gland that produces thyroxine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `thyroid (gland) (1)\nDo not accept: thymus / thyroxine gland / pituitary` },
      { q: `During a long football match, the concentration of glucose in a player's blood starts to fall. The pancreas releases a hormone that causes stored glycogen in the liver to be converted back into glucose.\n\nName this hormone.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `glucagon (1)\nDo not accept: glycogen / insulin\nIgnore: spelling if unambiguous, but do not accept 'glycogon'` },
      { q: `This question is about the menstrual cycle.\n\nIn a 28-day menstrual cycle, an egg is released from an ovary at about day 14.\n\nGive the name of this process.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `ovulation (1)\nDo not accept: menstruation / fertilisation` },
      { q: `A nurse tested urine samples using test strips that change colour if glucose is present. The urine of a healthy person gave a negative result for glucose, even though glucose is present in the filtrate in the Bowman's capsule.\n\nGive one reason why the urine of a healthy person contains no glucose.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `all of the glucose is (selectively) reabsorbed (into the blood) (1)\nAccept: glucose is taken back into the blood in the (first coiled / proximal) tubule\nDo not accept: glucose is not filtered / glucose is too big to be filtered` },
      { q: `A cyclist is riding on a hot day. The temperature of the blood flowing through the brain rises above 37 °C.\n\nName the part of the brain that detects this change in blood temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `hypothalamus (1)\nDo not accept: pituitary (gland) / cerebellum / medulla` },
      { q: `Contraception can be hormonal or non-hormonal. A condom is a barrier method of contraception.\n\nGive one advantage of using a condom rather than taking the combined contraceptive pill.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• protects against sexually transmitted infections / STIs / named STI such as HIV or chlamydia (1)\n• no hormonal side effects (1)\n• only needs to be used at the time of sex / no need to remember to take a pill every day (1)\nAccept: available without a prescription\nIgnore: 'cheaper'\nDo not accept: 'more effective'` },
      { q: `A student's heart rate was measured while sitting quietly and again just before giving a talk to the class (data written for this practice question).\n\nResting heart rate = 72 beats per minute\nHeart rate before the talk = 96 beats per minute\n\nThe increase was caused by adrenaline.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the student's heart rate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `96 − 72 = 24 (1)\n(24 ÷ 72) × 100 = 33 (%) (1)\nAccept: 33.3 (%)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 133 (%)` },
      { q: `A person with an underactive thyroid gland produces less thyroxine than normal. People with this condition often gain body mass and feel cold, even when they do not eat more food than before.\n\nExplain why a person with a low concentration of thyroxine may gain body mass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (thyroxine controls) metabolic rate, so metabolic rate is lower / slower (1)\n• so less glucose / fat / energy from food is used (in respiration), so more is stored as fat (1)\nAccept: less food used up for respiration\nDo not accept: 'less energy is made'` },
      { q: `Figure 1 (described): a graph of hormone concentration against day of the menstrual cycle. The concentration of oestrogen rises steadily from day 5 and reaches a peak at about day 12, just before ovulation.\n\nDescribe the role of oestrogen in the menstrual cycle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• causes the lining of the uterus to (repair and) thicken / build up (1)\n• (high concentration) stimulates the release of LH (from the pituitary gland) (1)\n• inhibits the release of FSH (1)\nAccept: makes the uterus wall thicker\nDo not accept: oestrogen causes ovulation\nIgnore: references to secondary sexual characteristics` },
      { q: `If an egg is not fertilised, the corpus luteum in the ovary breaks down and the concentration of progesterone in the blood falls at about day 26 of the menstrual cycle.\n\nExplain what happens to the lining of the uterus when the concentration of progesterone falls.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• progesterone maintains the lining of the uterus (1)\n• (so when it falls) the lining breaks down / is shed / menstruation (starts) (1)\nAccept: period starts\nDo not accept: the lining thickens` },
      { q: `The combined contraceptive pill contains the hormones oestrogen and progesterone. A woman takes the pill every day for 21 days of her cycle.\n\nExplain how the oestrogen in the pill prevents pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• oestrogen inhibits the release of FSH (from the pituitary gland) (1)\n• so follicles / eggs do not mature (and no egg is released) (1)\nAccept: so ovulation does not happen for the second mark\nDo not accept: 'kills eggs' / 'kills sperm'` },
      { q: `A contraceptive implant is a small rod placed under the skin of the arm. It releases the hormone progesterone slowly into the blood for up to three years.\n\nGive two ways that progesterone can prevent pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• inhibits the release of FSH / LH so eggs do not mature / ovulation does not happen (1)\n• makes the mucus in the cervix thicker / stickier so sperm cannot pass through (1)\n• makes the lining of the uterus thinner so an embryo cannot implant (1)\nDo not accept: kills sperm` },
      { q: `Table 1 shows how many women out of every 100 using a method of contraception become pregnant within one year (data written for this practice question).\n\nMethod | Pregnancies per 100 women per year\nmale condom | 18\ncombined pill | 9\nimplant | less than 1\n\nA clinic has 500 women who use male condoms as their only method of contraception.\n\nCalculate the number of these women who would be expected to become pregnant within one year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `18 ÷ 100 × 500 / 0.18 × 500 (1)\n= 90 (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Some women are unable to become pregnant because they do not ovulate. A doctor prescribes the drug clomifene for one of these women.\n\nExplain how clomifene therapy can help this woman to become pregnant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• clomifene increases the release of FSH and LH (from the pituitary gland) (1)\n• (FSH) stimulates eggs to mature and (LH) stimulates ovulation / egg release, so the egg can be fertilised (1)\nAccept: stops oestrogen inhibiting FSH release for the first mark\nDo not accept: clomifene is FSH / clomifene fertilises the egg` },
      { q: `In vitro fertilisation (IVF) can help couples who cannot conceive naturally. The woman is given hormones so that several eggs mature in her ovaries. The eggs are then collected from her ovaries.\n\nDescribe what happens to the eggs after they are collected and before they are placed in the woman's uterus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (eggs are mixed with) sperm (from the man / donor) in a dish (in a laboratory) so fertilisation occurs (1)\n• (fertilised eggs) develop into embryos / divide into balls of cells (for a few days) (1)\nAccept: sperm injected into the egg for the first mark\nIgnore: embryos checked / screened` },
      { q: `Homeostasis keeps internal conditions in the body constant. The core body temperature of a healthy person stays close to 37 °C.\n\nExplain why it is important that core body temperature does not rise to 42 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• enzymes (in the body) would denature / active site changes shape (1)\n• so (metabolic) reactions slow down / stop / substrate no longer fits the active site (1)\nAccept: enzymes have an optimum temperature of about 37 °C for the first mark\nDo not accept: enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `A hiker is walking in snow. Her skin looks pale because less blood is flowing through the capillaries near the surface of her skin.\n\nExplain how this change helps to reduce heat loss from her body.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (blood vessels / arterioles supplying skin capillaries) narrow / vasoconstriction (1)\n• so less heat is transferred / lost (by radiation) from the blood to the surroundings through the skin (1)\nDo not accept: capillaries move away from the skin surface / capillaries constrict` },
      { q: `A person drank a glucose drink. Their blood glucose concentration increased and the pancreas released insulin. Over the next two hours the blood glucose concentration returned to normal.\n\nDescribe how insulin causes the blood glucose concentration to decrease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (insulin causes) glucose to move / be taken up from the blood into cells (1)\n• glucose is converted to glycogen in the liver / muscles (1)\nAccept: glucose stored as glycogen in the liver\nDo not accept: insulin converts glucose to glucagon / insulin breaks down glucose` },
      { q: `A 12-year-old boy is diagnosed with type 1 diabetes. He is told that he will need to inject insulin several times each day.\n\nExplain why a person with type 1 diabetes needs to inject insulin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the pancreas does not produce (enough) insulin (1)\n• so blood glucose concentration would rise too high / cannot be lowered (after meals) (1)\nAccept: cells in the pancreas that make insulin have been destroyed\nDo not accept: body cells do not respond to insulin (this is type 2)` },
      { q: `Body mass index (BMI) is used to assess whether a person is at risk of type 2 diabetes.\n\nBMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²\n\nBMI | category\n18.5–24.9 | healthy weight\n25.0–29.9 | overweight\n30.0 and above | obese\n\nA man has a mass of 95 kg and a height of 1.75 m.\n\nCalculate the BMI of this man and use the table to state his category.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `95 ÷ 1.75² = 95 ÷ 3.0625 = 31(.0) (1)\nobese (1)\nAllow: second mark for category consistent with an incorrect calculated BMI\nAccept: 31.02` },
      { q: `A doctor measures a patient to assess their risk of developing type 2 diabetes (data written for this practice question).\n\nWaist circumference = 102 cm\nHip circumference = 96 cm\n\nCalculate the waist:hip ratio of this patient. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `102 ÷ 96 (1)\n= 1.06 (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working\nAllow 1 mark for 1.0625 / 1.063 (not rounded to 2 decimal places)\nDo not accept: 0.94 (hip ÷ waist)` },
      { q: `A rugby player has a BMI of 31, which puts him in the obese category. His waist:hip ratio is 0.86, which is in the low-risk range for men.\n\nExplain why the waist:hip ratio may be a better indicator of this player's risk of type 2 diabetes than his BMI.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• BMI does not distinguish between muscle and fat / his high mass may be due to muscle (1)\n• waist:hip ratio shows the amount of fat stored around the abdomen / waist, which is (more strongly) linked to type 2 diabetes (1)\nAccept: muscle is denser / heavier than fat for the first mark` },
      { q: `The urinary system contains the kidneys, the ureters, the bladder and the urethra.\n\nDescribe the route taken by urine from where it is made to where it leaves the body.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (made in) kidney then (down the) ureter to the bladder (1)\n• (stored in the bladder then) leaves through the urethra (1)\nDo not accept: ureter and urethra in the wrong order (max 1)` },
      { q: `Table 2 shows the concentration of some substances in the blood plasma, in the filtrate in the Bowman's capsule and in the urine of a healthy person (data written for this practice question).\n\nSubstance | plasma (g per 100 cm³) | filtrate (g per 100 cm³) | urine (g per 100 cm³)\nprotein | 8.00 | 0.00 | 0.00\nglucose | 0.10 | 0.10 | 0.00\nurea | 0.03 | 0.03 | 2.00\n\nExplain why there is no protein in the filtrate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• protein molecules are (too) large (1)\n• so cannot pass (out of the glomerulus) through the capillary wall / filter / into the Bowman's capsule (1)\nDo not accept: protein is reabsorbed\nIgnore: protein is needed by the body` },
      { q: `In a healthy person, the concentration of urea in the blood plasma is 0.03 g per 100 cm³ and the concentration of urea in the urine is 2.00 g per 100 cm³ (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate how many times more concentrated urea is in the urine than in the blood plasma.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.00 ÷ 0.03 (1)\n= 67 (times) (1)\nAccept: 66.7 / 66.67\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `After a long run on a hot day, a runner's blood has a high solute concentration. The pituitary gland releases more of the hormone ADH.\n\nExplain how ADH affects the volume of urine produced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ADH makes the collecting duct more permeable to water (1)\n• so more water is reabsorbed into the blood, so a smaller volume of (concentrated) urine is produced (1)\nAccept: kidney tubules for collecting duct\nDo not accept: ADH absorbs water` },
      { q: `During kidney dialysis, a patient's blood flows on one side of a partially permeable membrane and dialysis fluid flows on the other side. The dialysis fluid contains glucose at the same concentration as normal blood plasma but contains no urea.\n\nExplain why the dialysis fluid contains glucose at the same concentration as normal blood plasma.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• so there is no concentration gradient for glucose / no net diffusion of glucose (1)\n• so glucose is not lost from the blood (1)\nAccept: so glucose stays in the blood\nDo not accept: so glucose is added to the blood / glucose is filtered` },
      { q: `A person eats a meal that contains much more protein than the body needs. The excess amino acids cannot be stored and are broken down. A waste product is then removed from the blood by the kidneys.\n\nDescribe how this waste product, urea, is produced in the body.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (excess) amino acids are broken down / deaminated in the liver (1)\n• (the nitrogen-containing / amino part) is converted to urea (and released into the blood) (1)\nAccept: ammonia is converted to urea\nDo not accept: urea is made in the kidneys` },
      { q: `Thyroxine is a hormone that controls the metabolic rate. The concentration of thyroxine in the blood is controlled by negative feedback involving the hormones TRH and TSH.\n\nExplain how negative feedback keeps the concentration of thyroxine in the blood constant when the concentration of thyroxine falls.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (low thyroxine detected by the) hypothalamus which releases TRH (1)\n• TRH causes the pituitary gland to release TSH (1)\n• TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to release more thyroxine (until the concentration returns to normal) (1)\nAccept: when thyroxine is high, TRH / TSH release is inhibited, as an alternative for any one mark\nDo not accept: thyroxine stimulates the thyroid gland` },
      { q: `A man walking home at night hears a loud noise behind him. His adrenal glands release adrenaline into the blood and his body is prepared for 'fight or flight'.\n\nExplain how adrenaline prepares the man's body for 'fight or flight'.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• increases heart rate (and blood pressure) (1)\n• increases blood flow to the muscles (1)\n• (causes the liver to) convert glycogen to glucose / increases blood glucose concentration (1)\n• so more oxygen and glucose delivered to the muscles for (faster) respiration (1)\nMax 3\nDo not accept: 'adrenaline gives energy' / 'adrenaline makes energy'` },
      { q: `FSH and LH are hormones released by the pituitary gland during the menstrual cycle.\n\nExplain the roles of FSH and LH in the menstrual cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• FSH causes a follicle / egg to mature (in the ovary) (1)\n• FSH stimulates the ovary to produce oestrogen (1)\n• (a surge / peak of) LH stimulates ovulation / the release of the egg (from the follicle) (1)\nAccept: LH causes the corpus luteum to form (which produces progesterone) as an alternative to either FSH mark\nMax 3\nDo not accept: FSH releases the egg` },
      { q: `Table 3 shows the percentage of IVF embryo transfers that resulted in a live birth for women of different ages (data written for this practice question).\n\nAge of woman (years) | live births per embryo transfer (%)\nunder 35 | 32\n35–37 | 25\n38–39 | 19\n40–42 | 11\n43–44 | 5\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Table 3. Use data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as age increases, the (percentage) success rate of IVF / live births decreases (1)\n• use of data e.g. 32% (under 35) falls to 5% (43–44) / decrease of 27 percentage points (1)\n• manipulation of data e.g. women under 35 are 6.4 times more likely to have a live birth than women aged 43–44 / the success rate falls faster at older ages (1)\nDo not accept: 'negative correlation' alone with no reference to age and success` },
      { q: `Table 4 shows the percentage of adults in each BMI category who have type 2 diabetes (data written for this practice question).\n\nBMI category | adults with type 2 diabetes (%)\n18.5–24.9 | 2.5\n25.0–29.9 | 6.0\n30.0–34.9 | 11.5\n35.0 and above | 19.0\n\nA newspaper headline stated: 'Being obese causes type 2 diabetes'.\n\nEvaluate this headline using the data in Table 4.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• (as BMI increases) the percentage of adults with type 2 diabetes increases / positive correlation (1)\n• use of data e.g. 19.0% for BMI 35 and above compared with 2.5% for a healthy BMI / 7.6 times higher (1)\n• but correlation does not prove causation / other factors (such as age, genes, diet, lack of exercise) may be involved / some people with a healthy BMI have type 2 diabetes / obesity is a risk factor (1)` },
      { q: `A man has untreated type 2 diabetes. His blood glucose concentration is often very high, and a urine test shows glucose in his urine. In a healthy person, all the glucose in the filtrate is reabsorbed in the first coiled tubule of the nephron.\n\nExplain why glucose is found in the urine of this man.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• his cells do not respond to insulin, so the blood glucose concentration is high (1)\n• so the filtrate (in the Bowman's capsule / nephron) contains a high concentration of glucose (1)\n• not all of the glucose can be reabsorbed (into the blood), so some passes to the urine (1)\nAccept: reabsorption is at its maximum / carriers are all in use\nDo not accept: glucose is not filtered` },
      { q: `A hospital compared the costs of treating a patient with kidney failure by dialysis or by a kidney transplant (data written for this practice question).\n\nDialysis: £30 000 per year\nKidney transplant: £17 000 for the operation, then £5000 per year for immunosuppressant drugs\n\nCalculate how much money would be saved over 5 years by giving one patient a kidney transplant instead of dialysis.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• dialysis: 30 000 × 5 = £150 000 (1)\n• transplant: 17 000 + (5000 × 5) = £42 000 (1)\n• saving = 150 000 − 42 000 = £108 000 (1)\nAward 3 marks for the correct answer with no working\nAllow ECF for the final mark` },
      { q: `A runner's face becomes red during a race on a hot day.\n\nExplain how this change helps to keep the runner's body temperature constant.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (arterioles / blood vessels supplying the skin) widen / dilate / vasodilation (1)\n• more blood flows through the capillaries near the surface of the skin (1)\n• so more heat is transferred / lost (by radiation) from the blood to the surroundings (1)\nDo not accept: capillaries move closer to the skin surface\nDo not accept: blood vessels expand (for the first mark) unless qualified as widening` },
      { q: `Two people, P and Q, did not eat for 12 hours. Each person then drank a solution containing 75 g of glucose. Their blood glucose concentrations were measured every 30 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime (minutes) | 0 | 30 | 60 | 90 | 120\nP (mg per 100 cm³) | 85 | 140 | 120 | 95 | 88\nQ (mg per 100 cm³) | 130 | 210 | 260 | 250 | 230\n\nPerson Q has diabetes.\n\nExplain the differences between the results for person P and person Q.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Q has a higher blood glucose concentration at all times / starts higher (130 compared with 85) (1)\n• P's blood glucose peaks (at 30 minutes) then returns to near the starting value by 120 minutes but Q's continues to rise (to 260 at 60 minutes) and stays high (1)\n• (in P) insulin is released (by the pancreas), so glucose is taken up by cells / converted to glycogen in the liver / muscles (1)\n• Q does not produce (enough) insulin / Q's cells do not respond to insulin, so glucose is not removed from the blood (1)\nAccept: correct use of other data from the table` },
      { q: `A student wanted to investigate how sweating helps to cool the body. The student used two boiling tubes of hot water as models of the body. One tube was wrapped in a dry paper towel and the other in a paper towel soaked in water.\n\nDevise a method the student could use to find out whether the wet paper towel increases the rate of cooling.\n\nYou may use standard laboratory equipment.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `any four from:\n• put the same volume of hot water in each tube (1)\n• same starting temperature of water (e.g. 60 °C) (1)\n• measure the temperature with a thermometer at regular intervals / every minute for a set time (e.g. 10 minutes) (1)\n• control another variable e.g. same type / size of paper towel, same room temperature, tubes away from draughts (1)\n• compare the temperature fall / rate of cooling of the two tubes (1)\n• repeat (and calculate a mean) (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A woman runs a long-distance race on a hot day. During the race she sweats a lot and does not drink any water.\n\n*Explain how her body responds to keep both her core body temperature and the water content of her blood constant.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nTemperature\n• increase in blood temperature detected by (thermoregulatory centre in the) hypothalamus\n• sweat glands produce more sweat\n• water in sweat evaporates from the skin, transferring heat energy away from the body\n• vasodilation – arterioles supplying the skin widen\n• more blood flows near the skin surface so more heat is lost by radiation\nWater content\n• loss of water in sweat increases the solute concentration of the blood\n• detected by the hypothalamus\n• pituitary gland releases more ADH\n• ADH carried in the blood to the kidneys\n• collecting ducts become more permeable to water\n• more water reabsorbed into the blood\n• small volume of concentrated urine\n• both are examples of negative feedback / homeostasis\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of both temperature control and water control, with clear links between the change, the detection and the response, using correct terms (e.g. vasodilation, collecting duct, permeability).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation of both processes with some links, or a detailed explanation of one process only.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about sweating, blood vessels or ADH with few links.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `Figure 2 (described): a graph of the concentrations of four hormones during a 28-day menstrual cycle. FSH rises at the start of the cycle. Oestrogen rises from day 5 to a peak just before day 14. LH shows a sharp peak at day 14. Progesterone rises after day 14, peaks at about day 21 and then falls.\n\n*Explain how the interactions of the hormones FSH, LH, oestrogen and progesterone control the menstrual cycle.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• FSH released from the pituitary gland at the start of the cycle\n• FSH causes a follicle / egg to mature in the ovary\n• FSH stimulates the ovary to release oestrogen\n• oestrogen causes the uterus lining to thicken / repair after menstruation\n• rising oestrogen inhibits FSH release\n• high oestrogen stimulates the release of LH\n• LH surge causes ovulation at about day 14\n• LH causes the follicle to become the corpus luteum\n• corpus luteum releases progesterone\n• progesterone maintains the uterus lining\n• progesterone inhibits the release of FSH and LH\n• if the egg is not fertilised the corpus luteum breaks down and progesterone falls\n• fall in progesterone causes the lining to break down / menstruation, and FSH is no longer inhibited so the cycle starts again\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of the interactions of all four hormones, including feedback between hormones, in a logical sequence linked to events in the ovary and uterus.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation of the roles of at least three hormones with some interactions between hormones.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about the effect of one or more hormones with no interactions.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '8': {
    name: 'Exchange and Transport in Animals',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about blood.\nA person cuts their finger. After a few minutes the bleeding stops because the blood clots.\n\nName the component of the blood that helps the blood to clot.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• platelets (1)\nAccept: cell fragments\nDo not accept: red blood cells / white blood cells / plasma` },
      { q: `This question is about the heart.\nThe right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood out of the heart to the lungs.\n\nName the blood vessel that carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• pulmonary artery (1)\nDo not accept: pulmonary vein / aorta` },
      { q: `A student set up a respirometer to investigate the rate of respiration of woodlice. The woodlice were placed on a gauze platform above some soda lime in a sealed boiling tube. A capillary tube containing a drop of coloured liquid was connected to the boiling tube.\n\nState why soda lime was placed in the boiling tube.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• to absorb the carbon dioxide (released by the woodlice) (1)\nAccept: removes CO₂\nIgnore: absorbs gases / absorbs oxygen` },
      { q: `A sprinter runs a 100 m race. During the race, some of the sprinter's muscle cells respire anaerobically.\n\nName the product of anaerobic respiration in human muscle cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• lactic acid (1)\nAccept: lactate\nDo not accept: ethanol / carbon dioxide / alcohol` },
      { q: `Cellular respiration takes place continuously in all living cells. Respiration is an exothermic reaction.\n\nState what is meant by an exothermic reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• a reaction that transfers (heat) energy to the surroundings (1)\nAccept: releases energy / gives out heat\nDo not accept: produces energy / makes energy` },
      { q: `Muscle cells in the leg are supplied with blood by a network of capillaries. Oxygen diffuses from the blood in the capillaries into the muscle cells.\n\nGive one waste substance that diffuses from the muscle cells into the blood in the capillaries.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (1)\nAccept: CO₂ / lactic acid\nIgnore: water\nDo not accept: oxygen / glucose / urea` },
      { q: `Scientists use cubes of agar jelly as models of organisms of different sizes (data written for this practice question).\nOne agar cube has sides of length 4 cm.\n\nCalculate the surface area : volume ratio of this cube.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• surface area = 6 × 4 × 4 = 96 (cm²) and volume = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 (cm³) (1)\n• ratio = 96 : 64 = 1.5 : 1 (1)\nAccept: 3 : 2\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Amoeba is a single-celled organism that lives in ponds. It takes in oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide across its cell membrane.\n\nExplain why Amoeba does not need a transport system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Amoeba has a large surface area : volume ratio (1)\n• (so) diffusion across the cell membrane is enough to supply the whole cell / short diffusion distance to all parts of the cell (1)\nAccept: substances can diffuse quickly enough to meet its needs` },
      { q: `The human lungs contain about 480 million alveoli. Each alveolus is surrounded by a network of capillaries.\n\nExplain how having a very large number of alveoli helps gas exchange in the lungs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (gives a) large surface area (1)\n• (so) more oxygen / carbon dioxide can diffuse at the same time / faster rate of diffusion (1)\nIgnore: more gas exchange (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Emphysema is a lung disease. In a person with emphysema, the walls between alveoli break down. This produces fewer, larger air spaces in the lungs.\nPeople with emphysema often feel tired and out of breath.\n\nExplain why a person with emphysema feels tired.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (smaller) surface area for gas exchange / less oxygen diffuses into the blood (1)\n• (so) less (aerobic) respiration in cells / less energy transferred (1)\nAccept: less oxygen reaches muscle cells for respiration` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nFick's law states that the rate of diffusion is proportional to:\nsurface area × difference in concentration ÷ thickness of membrane\nIn a lung infection, fluid collects in the alveoli. This increases the diffusion distance from the air to the blood from 1 µm to 3 µm (data written for this practice question). The surface area and the difference in concentration stay the same.\n\nUse Fick's law to describe the effect of this change on the rate of diffusion of oxygen into the blood.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• rate of diffusion decreases (1)\n• to one third (of the original rate) (1)\nAccept: falls by 67% / falls by two thirds\nAward 1 mark for "rate decreases because the distance is greater" with no quantitative change` },
      { q: `Red blood cells have a biconcave disc shape. This means each cell is thinner in the centre than at the edges.\n\nExplain how the biconcave shape of a red blood cell helps it to carry out its function.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• large surface area (to volume ratio) / short diffusion distance (1)\n• (so) oxygen diffuses into / out of the cell quickly (1)\nIgnore: more room for haemoglobin` },
      { q: `Mature red blood cells in humans do not have a nucleus. They contain large amounts of the protein haemoglobin.\n\nExplain why red blood cells not having a nucleus is an advantage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• more space / room for haemoglobin (1)\n• (so) can carry more oxygen (1)\nDo not accept: more room for oxygen (for the first mark)` },
      { q: `Lymphocytes and phagocytes are two types of white blood cell.\n\nDescribe how each type of white blood cell protects the body against pathogens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• lymphocytes produce antibodies (1)\n• phagocytes engulf / ingest and digest pathogens (1)\nAccept: phagocytosis\nDo not accept: lymphocytes eat pathogens` },
      { q: `A doctor measures the blood pressure in a person's aorta. The pressure rises each time the left ventricle contracts.\n\nExplain why the aorta has a thick wall containing elastic fibres.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to withstand / cope with the high pressure (of blood from the ventricle) (1)\n• elastic fibres let the wall stretch and recoil / maintain pressure / even out the flow (1)\nIgnore: strong wall (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A student pressed gently on a vein on the inside of their wrist and then slid their finger towards their hand. The vein stayed empty between the finger and a point further down the arm.\n\nExplain why veins contain valves.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (blood in veins is at) low pressure (1)\n• valves prevent the backflow of blood / keep blood flowing towards the heart (1)` },
      { q: `A student dissected a pig's heart. The wall of the left ventricle was about 15 mm thick. The wall of the right ventricle was about 5 mm thick.\n\nExplain why the wall of the left ventricle is thicker than the wall of the right ventricle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• left ventricle pumps blood around the (whole) body / to the aorta; right ventricle pumps blood (only) to the lungs (1)\n• (so) left ventricle needs more muscle / produces higher pressure / more forceful contraction (1)` },
      { q: `Humans have a double circulatory system. Deoxygenated blood returns to the heart from the body in the vena cava.\n\nDescribe the route the blood takes from the vena cava until it leaves the heart.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (vena cava →) right atrium → right ventricle (1)\n• (right ventricle →) pulmonary artery (to the lungs) (1)\nAccept: passes through a valve between atrium and ventricle\nMax 1 if any chamber on the left side is included` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nThe equation for cardiac output is:\ncardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\nA man has a resting heart rate of 72 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 70 cm³.\n\nCalculate the man's cardiac output.\nGive the unit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 70 × 72 = 5040 (1)\n• cm³ per minute (1)\nAccept: 5.04 dm³ per minute (2)\nAccept: cm³/min, cm³ min⁻¹` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nA woman's cardiac output during gentle exercise is 6.3 dm³ per minute. Her heart rate is 90 beats per minute.\ncardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\n\nCalculate the woman's stroke volume in cm³.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 6.3 dm³ = 6300 cm³ / stroke volume = cardiac output ÷ heart rate (1)\n• 6300 ÷ 90 = 70 (cm³) (1)\nAward 1 mark for 0.07 (dm³)` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer containing germinating peas and soda lime. After 10 minutes, the drop of coloured liquid in the capillary tube had moved towards the peas.\n\nExplain why the coloured liquid moved towards the peas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• peas use / take in oxygen (for aerobic respiration) (1)\n• carbon dioxide released is absorbed by soda lime, so the volume of gas / pressure in the tube decreases (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the rate of respiration of germinating seeds using a respirometer. The student also set up a second respirometer that contained glass beads instead of seeds.\n\nExplain why the student set up the respirometer containing glass beads.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• as a control (1)\n• to show any movement of the liquid is caused by the seeds / respiration and not by changes in temperature or pressure (1)\nAccept: so that the results can be compared` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration of maggots using a respirometer in a water bath.\n\nState two variables that should be controlled in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• mass / number of maggots (1)\n• type / age of maggots (1)\n• mass of soda lime (1)\n• time the liquid is allowed to move for / time of acclimatisation (1)\n• volume of the tube / diameter of the capillary tube (1)\nDo not accept: temperature` },
      { q: `A footballer's leg muscles respire aerobically while walking and anaerobically during a sprint.\n\nGive two differences between aerobic respiration and anaerobic respiration in muscle cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• aerobic needs oxygen; anaerobic does not (1)\n• aerobic transfers more energy (per glucose molecule) (1)\n• aerobic produces carbon dioxide and water; anaerobic produces lactic acid (1)\nA difference must be comparative or describe both types` },
      { q: `Bread is made from flour, water, sugar and yeast. The dough is left in a warm place for an hour before it is baked. The volume of the dough increases.\n\nExplain why the volume of the dough increases.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• yeast respires (anaerobically) (1)\n• producing carbon dioxide (gas, which forms bubbles in the dough) (1)\nAccept: fermentation for the first mark\nIgnore: ethanol` },
      { q: `Metabolic rate can be estimated by measuring how much oxygen an animal uses in a given time.\nA scientist found that, per gram of body mass, a mouse uses much more oxygen than an elephant.\n\nExplain why a mouse uses more oxygen per gram of body mass than an elephant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• mouse has a larger surface area : volume ratio / loses heat faster (1)\n• (so) needs a higher rate of respiration / higher metabolic rate to release heat / keep its body temperature constant (1)` },
      { q: `After a 400 m race, an athlete continued to breathe deeply and quickly for several minutes, even though they were resting.\n\nExplain why the athlete continued to breathe deeply and quickly after the race.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to repay the oxygen debt / take in extra oxygen (1)\n• to break down lactic acid (that built up during anaerobic respiration) (1)\nAccept: to oxidise lactic acid / convert lactic acid to carbon dioxide and water` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to measure the rate of respiration of woodlice at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nDistance moved by the coloured liquid in 5 minutes:\n10 °C — 8 mm\n15 °C — 13 mm\n20 °C — 19 mm\n25 °C — 26 mm\n30 °C — 31 mm\n\nDescribe the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration of the woodlice.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as temperature increases, the rate of respiration increases (1)\n• use of data, e.g. distance increases from 8 mm at 10 °C to 31 mm at 30 °C / increases by 5–7 mm for every 5 °C rise (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated diffusion using agar cubes containing an indicator. The cubes were placed in acid. The indicator changed colour as the acid diffused into the cube (data written for this practice question).\nCube side length 1 cm — time for whole cube to change colour 2 minutes\nCube side length 2 cm — 7 minutes\nCube side length 3 cm — 15 minutes\n\nCalculate the surface area : volume ratio of the 2 cm cube and explain why the 3 cm cube took the longest time to change colour.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 24 : 8 = 3 : 1 (1)\n• 3 cm cube has the smallest surface area : volume ratio (2 : 1) (1)\n• (so) greater distance for acid to diffuse to the centre / less surface for each unit volume (1)` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nA student measured their pulse at rest. They counted 21 beats in 15 seconds. Their stroke volume is 75 cm³.\ncardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\n\nCalculate the student's cardiac output in cm³ per minute.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• heart rate = 21 × 4 = 84 (beats per minute) (1)\n• 75 × 84 (1)\n• = 6300 (cm³ per minute) (1)\nAllow ecf from an incorrect heart rate\nAward 3 marks for a correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to measure the volume of oxygen taken in by germinating peas. The drop of coloured liquid moved 24 mm along the capillary tube in 10 minutes. The radius of the capillary tube was 0.5 mm.\nVolume of gas = π r² × distance moved\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen uptake by the peas in mm³ per minute.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• volume = π × 0.5² × 24 (1)\n• = 18.8(5) (mm³) (1)\n• rate = 18.85 ÷ 10 = 1.9 (mm³ per minute) (1)\nAllow ecf for the division step` },
      { q: `A student repeated a respirometer experiment four times at 20 °C (data written for this practice question).\nDistance moved by the liquid in 5 minutes: 12 mm, 14 mm, 21 mm, 13 mm\n\nIdentify the anomalous result and calculate the mean distance moved, excluding the anomalous result.\nSuggest one reason for the anomalous result.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• anomalous result = 21 mm (1)\n• mean = (12 + 14 + 13) ÷ 3 = 13 (mm) (1)\n• suggestion, e.g. the tube was not sealed / temperature of the water bath changed / timing error / more or larger organisms used / liquid not reset to the start (1)` },
      { q: `Gas exchange happens in the alveoli. Breathing in and out (ventilation) brings fresh air into the alveoli, and blood flows continuously through the capillaries around them.\n\nExplain how ventilation and blood flow increase the rate of oxygen diffusion into the blood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ventilation keeps a high concentration of oxygen in the alveoli (1)\n• blood flow carries oxygenated blood away / brings deoxygenated blood with a low oxygen concentration (1)\n• (together these) maintain a steep concentration gradient (so diffusion is faster) (1)` },
      { q: `Some athletes train at high altitude, where the air contains less oxygen. After several weeks, the number of red blood cells in each cm³ of their blood increases.\n\nExplain how this change could improve the athlete's performance when they return to sea level.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• more haemoglobin (1)\n• (so) more oxygen carried (to muscles) (1)\n• (so) more aerobic respiration / more energy transferred / less anaerobic respiration / less lactic acid build-up (1)` },
      { q: `During a 200 m sprint, an athlete's heart and lungs cannot supply oxygen to the leg muscles fast enough. By the end of the race the athlete's leg muscles are painful and tired.\n\nExplain why the athlete's leg muscles become tired during the sprint.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (muscle cells) respire anaerobically (1)\n• (producing) lactic acid, which builds up in the muscles (1)\n• less energy is transferred (per glucose) than in aerobic respiration (1)\nAccept: lactic acid causes muscle fatigue / cramp` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nAn athlete has a resting cardiac output of 5000 cm³ per minute. Before training, the athlete's resting stroke volume was 70 cm³. After a year of training, the resting stroke volume increased to 100 cm³. The resting cardiac output stayed the same.\ncardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\n\nCalculate the decrease in the athlete's resting heart rate.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• heart rate before = 5000 ÷ 70 = 71.4 (beats per minute) (1)\n• heart rate after = 5000 ÷ 100 = 50 (beats per minute) (1)\n• decrease = 21 (beats per minute) (1)\nAccept: 22 if 72 − 50 is used after rounding\nAllow ecf` },
      { q: `Germinating seeds release energy by respiration.\n\nDevise a method to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration of germinating seeds.\nYou may use standard laboratory equipment and: a respirometer (a boiling tube connected to a capillary tube containing a drop of coloured liquid), soda lime, germinating seeds, water baths.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `• place a known mass / number of seeds on gauze above soda lime in the respirometer (1)\n• place in a water bath at one temperature and leave to acclimatise (1)\n• measure the distance moved by the coloured liquid in a set time, e.g. 5 minutes (1)\n• repeat at a range of temperatures, e.g. 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 °C, with the same mass of seeds (1)\nAccept: repeat at each temperature and calculate a mean / use a control tube with glass beads as alternatives to the last marking point` },
      { q: `Higher tier\nA scientist measured a person's cardiac output at rest and during hard exercise (data written for this practice question).\nAt rest: 5.2 dm³ per minute\nDuring hard exercise: 18.2 dm³ per minute\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in cardiac output and explain why cardiac output increases during exercise.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (18.2 − 5.2) ÷ 5.2 × 100 (1)\n• = 250 (%) (1)\n• muscles need more oxygen / glucose for (aerobic) respiration / more energy transferred for contraction (1)\n• carbon dioxide / heat / lactic acid needs to be removed faster (1)\nAccept: heart rate and / or stroke volume increase for neither explanation mark` },
      { q: `*Arteries, veins and capillaries carry blood around the body.\n\nExplain how the structures of arteries, veins and capillaries are related to their functions.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• arteries carry blood away from the heart at high pressure\n• arteries have thick walls with muscle and elastic fibres to withstand high pressure\n• elastic fibres stretch and recoil to keep blood flowing / maintain pressure\n• narrow lumen in arteries helps maintain pressure\n• veins carry blood back to the heart at low pressure\n• veins have thin walls and a wide lumen so blood flows easily with little resistance\n• veins have valves to prevent backflow\n• capillaries have walls one cell thick for a short diffusion distance\n• capillaries are narrow and form networks close to cells, giving a large surface area for exchange of oxygen, glucose and carbon dioxide\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation linking the structure of all three vessels to their functions; logical, well-linked reasoning.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation linking some structures to functions for at least two vessels; some linked reasoning.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple descriptions of the structure or function of the vessels with few links.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` },
      { q: `*A runner breathes in air containing oxygen. The oxygen is used by cells in the runner's leg muscles.\n\nExplain how oxygen gets from the air in the lungs to the cells in the leg muscles.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood in the capillaries\n• down a concentration gradient\n• alveoli have a large surface area, thin walls (one cell thick) and a good blood supply\n• oxygen enters red blood cells and combines with haemoglobin\n• oxygenated blood travels in the pulmonary vein to the left atrium\n• left ventricle contracts and pumps blood into the aorta at high pressure; valves prevent backflow\n• blood is carried in arteries to the leg muscles\n• in capillaries in the muscle, oxygen is released from haemoglobin and diffuses into muscle cells (one-cell-thick capillary walls, short distance)\n• muscle cells use oxygen in aerobic respiration, keeping the oxygen concentration in the cells low\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation covering exchange in the lungs, transport by the blood and heart, and exchange at the muscle, with logical sequencing.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation covering at least two of these stages with some linked reasoning.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about diffusion, the blood or the heart with few links.\n0 marks: No rewardable material.` }
    ]
  },
  '9': {
    name: 'Ecosystems and Material Cycles',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about a pond ecosystem.\n\nA pond contains populations of frogs, pondweed, water snails, dragonfly larvae and many other species.\n\nGive the term used to describe all the populations of different species living in the pond.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `community (1)\nDo not accept ecosystem / population / habitat` },
      { q: `Tapeworms live in the small intestine of humans. A tapeworm absorbs digested food from the human's intestine. The human gains nothing from the tapeworm.\n\nState the type of relationship between the tapeworm and the human.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `parasitism / parasitic (1)\nAccept the tapeworm is a parasite\nDo not accept mutualism / predation` },
      { q: `A student wanted to estimate the number of buttercup plants in a school field.\n\nThe student placed 10 quadrats in the field and counted the number of buttercup plants in each quadrat.\n\nGive one reason why the student should place the quadrats at random positions in the field.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• to avoid bias (in choosing where to sample) (1)\n• so that the sample is representative of the whole field (1)\nIgnore to make it fair / to make it accurate unqualified` },
      { q: `Some countries in the Middle East have very little rainfall and very little fresh water.\n\nThese countries produce potable water by removing the salt from sea water.\n\nName this process.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `desalination (1)\nAccept distillation / reverse osmosis\nIgnore filtration / evaporation alone` },
      { q: `Lichens grow on the bark of trees. Scientists counted the number of different species of lichen growing on trees in two areas.\n\nArea P had 14 species of lichen. Area Q had 2 species of lichen.\n\nState what these results suggest about the air in area P compared with the air in area Q.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `the air in area P is cleaner / less polluted / has a lower concentration of sulfur dioxide (than in area Q) (1)\nAccept converse for area Q\nIgnore less carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of fresh milk.\n\nThe student put a test tube containing milk, sodium carbonate solution and phenolphthalein indicator into a water bath at 30 °C. The student left the test tube in the water bath for 10 minutes before adding lipase solution.\n\nGive one reason why the student left the test tube in the water bath for 10 minutes before adding the lipase.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `so the milk mixture reaches the temperature of the water bath / reaches 30 °C (before the reaction starts) (1)\nAccept to make sure the temperature is the same as the water bath\nIgnore to make it a fair test unqualified` },
      { q: `Bluebells are plants that grow on the floor of woodlands. A student noticed that there were more bluebells in some parts of a woodland than in others.\n\nGive two abiotic factors that could affect the distribution of bluebells in the woodland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• light intensity (1)\n• temperature (1)\n• (soil) moisture / water content of soil (1)\n• (soil) pH (1)\nAccept mineral ions / nutrients in soil\nDo not accept biotic factors e.g. competition / grazing / trampling` },
      { q: `Red squirrels are native to the UK. Grey squirrels were introduced into the UK from North America in the 19th century.\n\nSince grey squirrels were introduced, the population of red squirrels in the UK has decreased.\n\nExplain why the introduction of grey squirrels caused the population of red squirrels to decrease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\n• grey squirrels compete with red squirrels for food / nuts / shelter / space / nesting sites (1)\n• grey squirrels are better competitors / eat more food / are larger (1)\n• so fewer red squirrels survive (to reproduce) (1)\n• grey squirrels carry a (pox) virus / disease that kills red squirrels (1)` },
      { q: `Snowshoe hares are eaten by lynx. Scientists recorded the populations of hares and lynx in a forest in Canada over several years.\n\nIn one year, the number of hares fell sharply. One year later, the number of lynx also fell.\n\nExplain why the number of lynx fell after the number of hares fell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• less food / fewer prey for the lynx (1)\n• so (more) lynx starve / die / fewer lynx reproduce (1)\nAccept increased competition between lynx for the hares for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Clover plants have swellings on their roots called root nodules. The root nodules contain nitrogen-fixing bacteria.\n\nThe relationship between the clover plant and the nitrogen-fixing bacteria is an example of mutualism.\n\nExplain why this relationship is described as mutualism.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the bacteria provide the plant with nitrogen compounds / nitrates / ammonium ions (1)\n• the plant provides the bacteria with sugars / carbohydrates / glucose / a place to live (1)\nAccept both organisms benefit for 1 mark if neither benefit is described` },
      { q: `Mistletoe is a plant that grows on the branches of trees such as apple trees. Mistletoe has roots that grow into the xylem and phloem of the tree.\n\nExplain why mistletoe is described as a parasite.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• mistletoe takes water / mineral ions / sugars from the tree (1)\n• the tree is harmed / does not benefit (1)\nAccept mistletoe benefits and the tree is harmed for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student used a belt transect to investigate the distribution of moss on the ground from the base of a hedge out into an open field.\n\nThe table shows the student's results (data written for this practice question).\n\nDistance from hedge (m) | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6 | 8 | 10\nPercentage cover of moss (%) | 80 | 65 | 40 | 20 | 10 | 5\n\nDescribe how the percentage cover of moss changes with distance from the hedge.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the percentage cover of moss decreases as the distance from the hedge increases (1)\n• use of data, e.g. falls from 80% at 0 m to 5% at 10 m / decreases most quickly between 2 m and 6 m / decreases by 75 percentage points (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the biomass at each trophic level in a grassland (data written for this practice question).\n\nProducers: 2400 g per m²\nPrimary consumers: 216 g per m²\nSecondary consumers: 26 g per m²\n\nCalculate the efficiency of biomass transfer from the producers to the primary consumers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (216 ÷ 2400) × 100 (1)\n• 9 (%) (1)\nAccept 9.0 (%)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nAward 1 mark for 0.09` },
      { q: `Rabbits eat grass. Only about 10% of the energy in the grass eaten by a rabbit population is transferred to the biomass of the rabbits.\n\nExplain why the rest of the energy is not transferred to the biomass of the rabbits.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\n• energy is used in / lost by respiration (1)\n• energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat (1)\n• (energy is used for) movement (1)\n• not all of the grass is digested / some is lost in faeces / egested (1)\n• some energy is lost in urine / excretion (1)\nIgnore energy used for growth` },
      { q: `This question is about a food chain in an oak woodland.\n\noak tree → aphids → ladybirds\n\nThe biomass at each trophic level is (data written for this practice question):\noak tree: 9000 g per m²\naphids: 600 g per m²\nladybirds: 50 g per m²\n\nDraw a pyramid of biomass for this food chain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• three stacked horizontal bars with the widest bar at the bottom, getting narrower to the top (1)\n• bars labelled oak tree (bottom), aphids (middle) and ladybirds (top) (1)\nAccept bars not drawn exactly to scale if the order of size is correct\nDo not accept an inverted pyramid or a pyramid of numbers shape` },
      { q: `Salmon are farmed in large net cages in sea lochs in Scotland. Thousands of salmon are kept in each cage and fed on pellets of food.\n\nGive two ways that fish farms like this can reduce biodiversity in the sea loch.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• uneaten food / fish faeces / waste can cause eutrophication (1)\n• diseases / parasites (e.g. sea lice) spread from farmed fish to wild fish (1)\n• chemicals / pesticides / antibiotics used on the fish kill other organisms (1)\n• farmed fish escape and compete with / breed with wild fish (1)\n• predators (e.g. seals) are killed to protect the farmed fish (1)` },
      { q: `In some parts of the UK, farmland is being replanted with native trees. This is called reforestation.\n\nGive two ways that reforestation can increase biodiversity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• provides habitats / shelter / nesting sites for animals (1)\n• provides food for (more) species / more food chains / food webs (1)\n• allows species to return / increases the number of different species / allows endangered species to recover (1)\n• allows species to move between areas / links up habitats (1)\n• reduces soil erosion / improves soil quality (for other plants) (1)` },
      { q: `Hedgerows are rows of shrubs and trees planted between fields. Some farmers removed hedgerows to make their fields larger. Conservation schemes now pay farmers to replant and look after hedgerows.\n\nExplain why replanting hedgerows can increase biodiversity on farmland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• hedgerows provide habitats / food / shelter / nesting sites (1)\n• for many different species, e.g. insects / birds / small mammals, so more species can live on the farm (1)\nAccept act as corridors allowing animals to move between areas for the second mark` },
      { q: `Food security means that all people have access to enough safe, nutritious food.\n\nThe human population of the world is increasing.\n\nGive two other factors that can threaten food security.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• changing diets (in wealthier populations) / eating more meat (1)\n• new pests / new pathogens (that attack crops or livestock) (1)\n• environmental change / climate change / drought / flooding (1)\n• using crops / land for biofuels (1)\n• cost of agricultural inputs, e.g. fertilisers / pesticides / fuel (1)\n• conflict / war (1)\nDo not accept increasing population` },
      { q: `In some countries, large areas of farmland are used to grow maize. The maize is used to make biofuel for cars.\n\nExplain how growing crops for biofuels can reduce food security.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• less land is available to grow (food) crops / the maize is not eaten (1)\n• so less food is produced / food prices increase (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about the carbon cycle.\n\nIn autumn, leaves fall from trees onto the ground in a woodland. After a few months, the leaves have decayed.\n\nDescribe how the carbon in the dead leaves is returned to the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• decomposers / microorganisms / bacteria / fungi feed on / break down the leaves (1)\n• carbon dioxide is released by respiration (of the decomposers) (1)\nAccept detritivores / earthworms for decomposers\nDo not accept combustion` },
      { q: `Large areas of tropical rainforest are being cut down and burned to clear land for farming.\n\nExplain how this deforestation increases the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• burning (the trees) releases carbon dioxide / combustion releases carbon dioxide (1)\n• fewer trees to remove / absorb carbon dioxide by photosynthesis (1)\nAccept decay of the cut trees by microorganisms releases carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `A field was flooded for several weeks. After the flood, the soil was waterlogged and contained very little oxygen.\n\nCrops grown in this soil grew poorly because the soil contained a low concentration of nitrates.\n\nExplain why waterlogged soil contains a low concentration of nitrates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any two from:\n• denitrifying bacteria (grow well in anaerobic conditions and) convert nitrates into nitrogen gas (1)\n• nitrifying bacteria need oxygen / cannot work without oxygen (1)\n• so less ammonia / fewer ammonium compounds are converted into nitrates (1)\nAccept nitrates are washed out of the soil / leached by the flood water (1)` },
      { q: `A gardener put 450 g of grass cuttings into a compost bin. After 6 weeks, the mass of the grass cuttings in the bin was 315 g.\n\nCalculate the rate of decay of the grass cuttings in grams per week.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (450 − 315) = 135 (g) (1)\n• 135 ÷ 6 = 22.5 (g per week) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nAccept 23 (g per week)` },
      { q: `Fresh meat can be vacuum packed. In vacuum packing, the air is removed from the packet before the packet is sealed.\n\nExplain why vacuum packing slows the decay of the meat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is no oxygen in the packet (1)\n• so microorganisms / bacteria / decomposers cannot respire (aerobically) / grow / reproduce (1)` },
      { q: `Students sampled invertebrates from a river at two sites. Site A was upstream of a pipe releasing untreated sewage. Site B was downstream of the pipe.\n\nThe table shows the numbers of each type of invertebrate found (data written for this practice question).\n\nInvertebrate | Site A | Site B\nstonefly nymph | 23 | 0\nfreshwater shrimp | 31 | 3\nsludge worm | 2 | 46\nrat-tailed maggot | 0 | 18\n\nUse the information in the table to explain which site has the more polluted water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• site B (1)\n• (more) sludge worms / rat-tailed maggots, which are found in polluted water / water with low oxygen\nOR\nfewer / no stonefly nymphs / freshwater shrimps, which need clean water / water with high oxygen (1)\nThe second mark must refer to data or to a named indicator species` },
      { q: `This question is about the water cycle.\n\nWater in the sea can eventually fall as rain on land.\n\nDescribe how water in the sea becomes rain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (heat from the Sun causes) water to evaporate (from the sea) and form water vapour (1)\n• water vapour cools and condenses to form clouds / droplets, which fall as rain / precipitation (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of fresh milk.\n\nThe student mixed milk, sodium carbonate solution and phenolphthalein indicator in a test tube. The mixture was pink. The student added lipase solution and timed how long it took for the pink colour to disappear.\n\nThe student repeated the investigation at different temperatures.\n\nGive two variables the student should control in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• volume of milk (1)\n• volume / concentration of lipase solution (1)\n• volume / concentration of sodium carbonate solution (1)\n• number of drops / volume of phenolphthalein (1)\n• type / source / age of milk, e.g. full-fat (1)\n• same end point / colour judged against the same background (1)\nDo not accept temperature` },
      { q: `A student wanted to estimate the number of dandelion plants in a field. The field is a rectangle 25 m long and 40 m wide.\n\nThe student placed 10 quadrats at random in the field. Each quadrat measured 0.5 m × 0.5 m.\n\nThe numbers of dandelions in the quadrats were (data written for this practice question):\n4, 7, 2, 0, 5, 3, 6, 1, 4, 8\n\nCalculate the estimated number of dandelion plants in the field.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean number per quadrat = 40 ÷ 10 = 4 (1)\n• area of quadrat = 0.25 m² so 4 ÷ 0.25 = 16 per m²\nOR area of field = 25 × 40 = 1000 m² (1)\n• 16 × 1000 = 16 000 (dandelions) (1)\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow ECF from an incorrect mean` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of fresh milk.\n\nThe student mixed milk, sodium carbonate solution and phenolphthalein indicator, then added lipase. Lipase breaks down fats in the milk into fatty acids and glycerol. The student recorded the time taken for the pink colour of the indicator to disappear.\n\nThe results are shown in the table (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature (°C) | 15 | 25 | 35 | 45 | 55\nTime for pink colour to disappear (s) | 900 | 420 | 210 | 360 | colour did not disappear\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• the colour disappears because fatty acids are produced, which lower the pH (1)\n• from 15 °C to 35 °C the time decreases / rate increases because molecules have more kinetic energy / more collisions between lipase and fat (1)\n• (about) 35 °C is (close to) the optimum temperature for lipase (1)\n• above 35 °C the time increases / rate decreases because the enzyme starts to denature / active site changes shape (1)\n• at 55 °C the lipase is denatured so no fatty acids are produced / substrate no longer fits the active site (1)` },
      { q: `In a woodland ecosystem, the producers trap 85 000 kJ of energy per m² per year (data written for this practice question).\n\nThe efficiency of energy transfer from the producers to the primary consumers is 12%.\nThe efficiency of energy transfer from the primary consumers to the secondary consumers is 15%.\n\nCalculate the energy transferred to the secondary consumers per m² per year.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 85 000 × 0.12 OR 85 000 × 12 ÷ 100 (1)\n• = 10 200 (kJ) (1)\n• 10 200 × 0.15 = 1530 (kJ per m² per year) (1)\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working\nAllow ECF from an incorrect first step` },
      { q: `Cane toads were introduced into Australia in 1935 to control beetles that were eating sugar cane crops. Cane toads are not native to Australia. They have no natural predators there and they produce a poison that kills many native animals that try to eat them, such as snakes and quolls.\n\nThe population of cane toads in Australia is now more than 200 million.\n\nExplain how the introduction of cane toads has affected biodiversity in Australia.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• biodiversity has decreased (1)\n• cane toads compete with native species for food / space / breeding sites (1)\n• predators (e.g. snakes / quolls) are poisoned / die when they eat cane toads, so their populations fall (1)\n• cane toads eat / prey on native species, e.g. insects / small animals (1)\n• no predators so the cane toad population grows quickly / spreads into new areas (1)\n• effects pass through food webs / other species that depend on the native animals are affected (1)` },
      { q: `Stonefly nymphs are only found in water with a high concentration of dissolved oxygen.\n\nA farmer allowed animal waste to drain into a stream. Stonefly nymphs that had lived in the stream disappeared from the part of the stream below where the waste entered.\n\nExplain why the stonefly nymphs disappeared from this part of the stream.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• bacteria / microorganisms / decomposers break down / feed on the waste (1)\n• the population of bacteria increases and they respire (aerobically) using oxygen (1)\n• so the concentration of dissolved oxygen falls / is too low for stonefly nymphs (to survive) (1)\nAccept waste causes eutrophication / algal growth, then plants die and are decomposed for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student wanted to investigate how the distribution of rushes changes from the edge of a pond into the surrounding field.\n\nThe student was given a 20 m tape measure and a 0.5 m × 0.5 m quadrat.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the distribution of rushes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• lay the tape measure out from the edge of the pond (in a straight line) into the field / to form a transect (1)\n• place the quadrat at regular intervals along the tape, e.g. every 2 m (1)\n• count the number of rushes / estimate percentage cover of rushes in each quadrat (1)\n• repeat with more transects / at other places around the pond and calculate a mean (1)\n• measure an abiotic factor, e.g. soil moisture, at each quadrat (1)` },
      { q: `A gardener noticed that grass cuttings in a compost bin decayed much faster in summer than in winter.\n\nThe gardener also turned the compost over with a fork every week and added water when it was dry.\n\nExplain how these conditions increase the rate of decay of the grass cuttings.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• higher temperature in summer increases the activity of enzymes in decomposers / microorganisms (1)\n• turning the compost adds oxygen / air, so the decomposers can respire (aerobically) (1)\n• water is needed for the decomposers to grow / reproduce / for enzymes to work (1)\nAccept higher temperature means decomposers reproduce faster` },
      { q: `In many countries, people are eating more meat as they become wealthier. Most of the animals farmed for meat are fed on crops such as soya and wheat.\n\nExplain why an increase in meat eating could reduce food security.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is an extra trophic level / humans are secondary consumers instead of primary consumers (1)\n• energy / biomass is lost between trophic levels, e.g. through respiration / heat / movement / waste (1)\n• so more land / more crops are needed to produce the same amount of food (for humans) / less food is available for people to eat (1)` },
      { q: `A farmer added nitrate fertiliser to a field next to a lake. Heavy rain washed some of the fertiliser into the lake.\n\nA few weeks later, the surface of the lake was covered with algae and many fish in the lake died.\n\nExplain how the fertiliser caused the fish to die.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• nitrates cause rapid growth of algae / algal bloom / eutrophication (1)\n• algae block light from reaching plants (under the surface) so the plants cannot photosynthesise and die (1)\n• bacteria / decomposers break down the dead plants / algae and their population increases (1)\n• the bacteria respire aerobically and use up the dissolved oxygen in the water (1)\n• fish do not have enough oxygen for respiration (so they die) (1)\nMaximum 4 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of carbon dioxide in the air of a woodland over 24 hours in summer.\n\nThe results are shown in the table (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime | 00:00 | 04:00 | 08:00 | 12:00 | 16:00 | 20:00\nCarbon dioxide concentration (parts per million) | 440 | 455 | 430 | 395 | 400 | 425\n\nExplain the changes in the concentration of carbon dioxide in the woodland over the 24 hours.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide concentration increases during the night / from 00:00 to 04:00 / is highest at 04:00 (1)\n• because there is no light, so no photosynthesis, but respiration continues (in plants / animals / decomposers) (1)\n• carbon dioxide concentration decreases during the day / is lowest at 12:00 (1)\n• because the rate of photosynthesis is greater than the rate of respiration / plants take in carbon dioxide (for photosynthesis) (1)\nAccept light intensity is greatest around midday for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `*Fresh milk decays when microorganisms break down the fats in the milk. Lipase is an enzyme that breaks down fats into fatty acids and glycerol.\n\nA student was given fresh full-fat milk, lipase solution, sodium carbonate solution, phenolphthalein indicator (pink in alkaline solution and colourless in neutral or acidic solution), test tubes, measuring cylinders, a thermometer, water baths and a stop clock.\n\nDescribe how the student could use this apparatus to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of decay of fresh milk.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nMethod\n• measure a set volume of milk, e.g. 5 cm³, into a test tube using a measuring cylinder\n• add a set volume of sodium carbonate solution, e.g. 7 cm³, and a set number of drops of phenolphthalein so the mixture is pink / alkaline\n• put the test tube and a separate tube of lipase solution in a water bath at the chosen temperature, e.g. 20 °C\n• leave until both reach the temperature of the water bath; check with a thermometer\n• add a set volume of lipase, e.g. 1 cm³, to the milk, mix and start the stop clock\n• stop the clock when the pink colour disappears / the mixture becomes colourless\n• repeat at a range of temperatures, e.g. 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 °C\n• repeat at each temperature and calculate a mean\nResults\n• the colour disappears because fatty acids are produced and lower the pH\n• the shorter the time, the faster the rate of decay; rate can be calculated as 1 ÷ time\n• plot a graph of rate (or time) against temperature\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate; a basic method, e.g. mix milk and lipase at different temperatures and see when the colour changes; lacks detail and structure.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant; a method that could be followed, including timing the colour change at different temperatures and some control variables, but some steps are missing or unclear.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout; a logical, detailed method including equilibrating to temperature, control of volumes, a range of temperatures, repeats and how the rate is found from the time for the colour to disappear.` },
      { q: `*Wheat plants need nitrates to grow well. A farmer grows wheat in a field every year.\n\nThe air contains about 78% nitrogen gas, but wheat plants cannot use nitrogen gas.\n\nExplain how nitrogen becomes available to wheat plants as nitrates, and how the farmer could increase the concentration of nitrates in the soil of the field.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nThe nitrogen cycle\n• nitrogen-fixing bacteria (in soil or in root nodules of legumes) convert nitrogen gas into ammonia / ammonium compounds\n• lightning can also fix nitrogen gas into nitrogen oxides / nitrates\n• when plants and animals die, or animals produce waste (urine / faeces), decomposers break down proteins into ammonia / ammonium compounds\n• nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium compounds into nitrites and then into nitrates\n• nitrifying bacteria need oxygen / well-aerated soil\n• nitrates are absorbed by root hair cells by active transport and used to make proteins / amino acids\n• denitrifying bacteria convert nitrates into nitrogen gas, especially in waterlogged / anaerobic soil\nIncreasing nitrates\n• add (artificial) fertiliser containing nitrates / ammonium compounds\n• add manure / compost, which is decomposed to release ammonium compounds that are nitrified\n• crop rotation: grow a legume (e.g. peas / beans / clover) in some years, as these contain nitrogen-fixing bacteria in root nodules\n• plough the legumes back into the soil so they are decomposed\n• drain / plough the soil to improve aeration, which helps nitrifying bacteria and reduces denitrification\n\nLevel 0 (0 marks): no rewardable material.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): demonstrates elements of biological understanding, some of which is inaccurate; e.g. bacteria make nitrates or the farmer adds fertiliser; limited links between points.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): demonstrates biological understanding which is mostly relevant; describes some stages of the nitrogen cycle with named types of bacteria and at least one way the farmer can increase nitrates, with some logical links.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): demonstrates accurate and relevant biological understanding throughout; a coherent explanation of nitrogen fixation, decomposition and nitrification leading to nitrate uptake, linked to two or more methods the farmer could use, with reasons.` }
    ]
  }
};
